It is this ephemeral, kind of,

261
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.240
it's humors lurking around in the body somewhere,

262
00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.280
but most of the immune system is physically located

263
00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.207
in your gut, about 70% to 80% of it,

264
00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:17.430
and so, when we talk about the immune system,

265
00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.200
we do a little Immunology 101,

266
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.200
and full disclosure, I'm not an immunologist,

267
00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.590
I'm a gastroenterologist, but let me give folks some basics.

268
00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.080
So, we're really talking about two systems.

269
00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.420
We're talking about the innate immune system

270
00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:31.253
that you're born with,

271
00:11:31.253 --> 00:11:33.300
and an acquired immune system,

272
00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.133
sometimes called the adaptive immune system,

273
00:11:35.133 --> 00:11:37.470
that you acquire over time.

274
00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.620
The innate immune system works quickly,

275
00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:41.940
but it's sort of non-specific.

276
00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.950
So, for example, if you get a cut,

277
00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.170
it works to help protect you from the bacteria

278
00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.780
that may be invading through that open wound,

279
00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.740
but in a very non-specific way.

280
00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:54.180
The acquired immune system develops over time

281
00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.714
and it actually keeps a record of every pathogen

282
00:11:57.714 --> 00:12:00.489
that you're exposed to, so that it can remember it

283
00:12:00.489 --> 00:12:01.680
and mount to response.

284
00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.390
So, it's sort of like that person who never forgets,

285
00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.107
and holds, a grudge, and is like, "Oh, yeah,

286
00:12:05.107 --> 00:12:07.650
"in first grade you kicked me onto the table."

287
00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.120
Like a crow, right?

288
00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:09.953
Crows never forget.

289
00:12:09.953 --> 00:12:11.130
Exactly.

290
00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:14.470
So, the acquired immune system is able to...

291
00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.457
It takes a little bit longer to work,

292
00:12:16.457 --> 00:12:19.290
and particularly if it's a new organism,

293
00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:23.280
but it remembers, and then it is able to release antibodies.

294
00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.113
So, the acquired immune system

295
00:12:24.113 --> 00:12:27.600
involves T lymphocytes and B lymphocytes,

296
00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.640
and the B ones make the antibodies

297
00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:30.480
and the T lymphocytes

298
00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.390
are kind of like air traffic control, destroying cells

299
00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:34.860
that have been infected, et cetera,

300
00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:38.640
but if you think about the adaptive immune system,

301
00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.641
the point that I'd like to make for people is

302
00:12:40.641 --> 00:12:44.280
that is a basis of vaccines, for example.

303
00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:49.020
A vaccine introduces a tiny little bit of the viral protein,

304
00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:51.300
not enough, ideally, to make you sick,

305
00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.282
but enough for your adaptive immune system

306
00:12:54.282 --> 00:12:56.850
to start to create antibodies against it

307
00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:00.480
for the next time it encounters that virus,

308
00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.330
and so, if you think about some of these things,

309
00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.940
like with measles for example, a virus that's very old,

310
00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.630
you get measles and you, you know,

311
00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.480
have the illness, your immune system remembers,

312
00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.700
so that the next time you're exposed to measles,

313
00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:15.533
you are immune.

314
00:13:15.533 --> 00:13:17.460
Now, vaccine can do the same thing, right?

315
00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:21.210
But so can having the disease in that particular instance,

316
00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:24.990
and so, what we see is that the adaptive immune system

317
00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.559
can make you less sick or not sick at all

318
00:13:27.559 --> 00:13:29.510
the next time you encounter something.

319
00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:30.480
Sure.

320
00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.920
So, the innate immune system, the acquired immune system.

321
00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.030
So, when we talk about, I think you said 70%

322
00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:39.810
of the immune system resides in the gut.

323
00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.810
So, what is it exactly that is in the gut?

324
00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:44.190
Is it these lymphocytes?

325
00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:46.343
Is it these, you know, systems and pathways?

326
00:13:46.343 --> 00:13:47.610
Like, what are we talking about?

327
00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:48.990
It's these actual cells.

328
00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.240
So, when you think about the gut lining,

329
00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.800
this is a razor thin lining.

330
00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.497
It is literally, you know, a millimeter or two thick,

331
00:13:55.497 --> 00:13:57.510
and it's a net, it's a permeable net,

332
00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.820
like a fishing net with tiny holes,

333
00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:01.275
and it allows some things to go through.

334
00:14:01.275 --> 00:14:04.239
So, typically digested food,

335
00:14:04.239 --> 00:14:07.650
digested into the micronutrients passes through,

336
00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:09.700
and then the excrement,

337
00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.620
the waste matter from cells passes out,

338
00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.480
and remember that when something is in your GI tract,

339
00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.160
it's not actually inside your body,

340
00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.220
it's in this long hollow tube from mouth to anus,

341
00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.990
and so, things get passed out.

342
00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.930
Well, the immune cells are on one side.

343
00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:26.700
They're on the inside part of the net,

344
00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:30.180
inside your body, and your trillions of microbes

345
00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.600
are on the outside, on the other side of the gut lining,

346
00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.190
and, of course, there's a thick mucus layer

347
00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.080
also surrounding the gut microbes,

348
00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.860
but it is literally this hand and glove relationship

349
00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.863
and there's constant communication.

350
00:14:42.863 --> 00:14:46.740
So, the gut bacteria actually guide that immune response.

351
00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:49.890
They're constantly sending signals to the immune cells

352
00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:50.723
to tell 'em what to do,

353
00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:54.180
and I'll give you, a great example of that is,

354
00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:57.330
there is a bacteria in the gut called bacteroidetes.

355
00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.490
It's a type of bacteria,

356
00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:01.230
and there are many different strains,

357
00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:05.370
and when certain viruses enter the GI tract,

358
00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:09.063
bacteroidetes in the gut lining right there, you know,

359
00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.005
in the lumen against the lining

360
00:15:12.005 --> 00:15:16.590
will trigger a message interferon,

361
00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:17.700
and they're called interferons

362
00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:19.571
because they interfere with viruses

363
00:15:19.571 --> 00:15:23.580
to release certain cytokines to destroy the viruses,

364
00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:27.150
and so, if your complimentive gut bacteria are off,

365
00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:28.780
and you don't have a healthy microbiome,

366
00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:30.425
and you don't have sufficient quantities,

367
00:15:30.425 --> 00:15:33.300
or the right strains of bacteroidetes,

368
00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.290
you might not trigger that release of interferons

369
00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.620
and the sort of virus slaying capabilities, if you will.

370
00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:43.260
So, it's both a functional relationship,

371
00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:45.480
and it's an actual physical relationship, Rich,

372
00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:47.377
because they're right there next to each other,

373
00:15:47.377 --> 00:15:50.700
constantly communicating, and when you think about it,

374
00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:53.370
your immune system, your gut lumen

375
00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.880
is exposed to trillions of different things,

376
00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.800
because, again, it's in contact with the environment,

377
00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:00.740
what we're eating and swallowing.

378
00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.600
Everything's going into this open tube,

379
00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.997
and your immune system has to look at this morass of,

380
00:16:06.997 --> 00:16:10.290
you know, a gazillion different organisms,

381
00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.730
and figure out what's friend and what's foe,

382
00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:18.480
and it's really the gut microbiome that is directing that,

383
00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:19.747
and that's literally saying, "Yeah,

384
00:16:19.747 --> 00:16:22.153
"this one, you need to be really worried.

385
00:16:22.153 --> 00:16:23.347
"Do something big.

386
00:16:23.347 --> 00:16:24.577
"This one, just ignore.

387
00:16:24.577 --> 00:16:26.430
"Yeah, this one's actually helping."

388
00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:28.400
So, it's that back and forth relationship,

389
00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:32.040
that communication that's happening all the time in our gut.

390
00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.830
So, essentially these things are completely interwoven.

391
00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:36.030
You can't talk about the gut

392
00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:39.130
without talking about the immune system, and vice versa.

393
00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:41.727
They're 100% dependent upon each other

394
00:16:41.727 --> 00:16:43.820
in terms of their functionality,

395
00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:48.060
and when we talk about immune system dysregulation,

396
00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:49.290
that can come in two forms.

397
00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:52.362
Either it's hyperactive, and then you see all of these,

398
00:16:52.362 --> 00:16:56.790
like, autoimmune diseases or allergies to foods, and such,

399
00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.460
the cytokine storm that comes with, you know,

400
00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:02.640
certain people who succumb to COVID, et cetera,

401
00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:05.700
or the underperforming immune system that you mentioned,

402
00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.040
where you're not producing the interferons

403
00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:09.690
and the cytokines necessary

404
00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:12.360
to combat the disease on the front lines,

405
00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.210
the virus on the front lines.

406
00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:14.493
Is that a fair--
Yeah, that's

407
00:17:14.493 --> 00:17:18.210
a beautiful summary, and I think even dividing it further

408
00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.503
into internal threats and external threats

409
00:17:20.503 --> 00:17:22.910
can help to clarify it even more,

410
00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:26.130
but you gave an a beautiful summary of what it is.

411
00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:30.050
So, if we think about an overactive immune system,

412
00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:31.920
on the one hand, I want people

413
00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.699
to think about internal threats,

414
00:17:33.699 --> 00:17:36.450
and that would manifest as autoimmune disease.

415
00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:39.570
So, that's basically when your body is reacting

416
00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:41.130
to your own body's normal tissue,

417
00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.453
it's recognizing your own joints, skin, et cetera,

418
00:17:44.453 --> 00:17:47.220
as "foreign," and mounting an immune response,

419
00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.740
and so, we see that with the autoimmune disease,

420
00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:50.980
effect one in four Americans,

421
00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:54.090
there are over 100 different autoimmune diseases now,

422
00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:56.853
and this is a list that's growing, unfortunately.

423
00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.270
We can talk about why that's happening later.

424
00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:02.098
When we look, still, in the category

425
00:18:02.098 --> 00:18:05.680
of an overactive immune system, we look at external threats,

426
00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.100
and so, that would be, people are having allergies,

427
00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.640
peanut allergies, severe allergies to bee stings.

428
00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:15.510
I was exploring the marsh Beaufort, South Carolina,

429
00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:17.700
my husband's hometown, back in May,

430
00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:22.323
and we had the unfortunate privilege

431
00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:27.510
of being bitten up by chiggers, these insects,

432
00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:29.850
and his healed in about two or three weeks,

433
00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:32.340
and mine are still active four months later.

434
00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.620
I've have this sort of unknown,

435
00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.890
delayed hypersensitivity reaction to it,

436
00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.511
and maybe because he grew up in South Carolina,

437
00:18:40.511 --> 00:18:43.080
and he's been bitten by chiggers before as a kid,

438
00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:44.694
and I never have, but that's an example

439
00:18:44.694 --> 00:18:48.817
of an exaggerated response to this external threat.

440
00:18:48.817 --> 00:18:52.140
So, that's all overactive immune system.

441
00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.820
Underactive immune system,

442
00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:55.638
if we, again, look at the two categories

443
00:18:55.638 --> 00:18:58.290
of internal threat and external threat,

444
00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.320
the internal threat would be people developing cancer

445
00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.550
because your immune system doesn't

446
00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:03.835
just protect you from infection,

447
00:19:03.835 --> 00:19:06.610
it also helps with cancer surveillance.

448
00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:09.060
So, as our cells start dividing,

449
00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:13.290
sometimes they start to divide a little precariously,

450
00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.620
and that reproduction leads to errors

451
00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:18.330
in the genetic material of the cell,

452
00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.787
and over time that can transform to cancer,

453
00:19:20.787 --> 00:19:23.067
and so, the internal cancer surveillance system

454
00:19:23.067 --> 00:19:24.865
is also something that our immune system does,

455
00:19:24.865 --> 00:19:26.940
and it would weed out those cells,

456
00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.860
so that they die off and they're not continuing

457
00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.260
to proliferate and form cancer.

458
00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:32.970
So, with an underactive immune system,

459
00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:33.906
your cancer surveillance is off,

460
00:19:33.906 --> 00:19:35.697
and you're at increased risk of the cancer

461
00:19:35.697 --> 00:19:38.760
on the internal side, and on the external side,

462
00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.650
that would be infection, viral,

463
00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:42.810
bacterial, fungal, et cetera.

464
00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:45.870
So, what what we are should all be aiming for is

465
00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:47.840
this concept of a Goldilocks immune system.

466
00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:52.233
An immune system that is active enough to clear a virus,

467
00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:55.860
but not so active that we end up with a cytokine storm.

468
00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:57.669
You mentioned this overblown immune response,

469
00:19:57.669 --> 00:19:59.161
and the really fascinating thing

470
00:19:59.161 --> 00:20:01.260
when we look at this pandemic

471
00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:03.630
is that a lot of the deaths and the illness

472
00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:06.420
have been due primarily to the immune response,

473
00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:07.780
not so much of virus itself,

474
00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:10.710
but it's our bodies unregulated

475
00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.579
or dysregulated response to that virus

476
00:20:13.579 --> 00:20:16.510
that's causing the acute respiratory distress syndrome

477
00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:20.070
or other severe illnesses, and sometimes even death.

478
00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:20.903
Yeah, sure.

479
00:20:20.903 --> 00:20:23.849
So, there's so much in what he just said to unpack,

480
00:20:23.849 --> 00:20:27.630
but to kind of pull some threads on this,

481
00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:32.630
I mean, first of all, yes, in this era of COVID,

482
00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:35.310
I think we can all agree

483
00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:37.461
that we've been on the receiving end

484
00:20:37.461 --> 00:20:42.461
of a lot of conflicting information and social vitriol

485
00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.440
that kind of swirled around, what is fact, what is fiction?

486
00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:46.830
Certainly, you know,

487
00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.963
one truth that was kind of underrepresented was

488
00:20:49.963 --> 00:20:51.838
that there was not enough messaging

489
00:20:51.838 --> 00:20:54.840
about the personal responsibility

490
00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:56.340
that we have for our own health,

491
00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:59.850
and much of what you talk about, and is in your book

492
00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:04.850
is the malleability, the adaptability of the gut microbiome,

493
00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.530
and, in turn, our immune system.

494
00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:09.690
When we sort of get rid of certain things

495
00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:12.090
or stop certain habits and adapt new habits,

496
00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.880
it really is resilient in that regard,

497
00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:19.050
and we're all capable of, you know,

498
00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:21.270
creating a Goldilocks immune system.

499
00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:23.370
I mean, most of the languaging is

500
00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:24.440
around boosting our immune system.

501
00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:25.950
Obviously, that's problematic

502
00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:26.970
for the reasons that you just said.

503
00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:28.725
It is this Goldilocks immune system

504
00:21:28.725 --> 00:21:31.470
that we're all striving for,

505
00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:34.050
but to kind of begin to understand this,

506
00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:35.970
I think we have to talk about,

507
00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:38.070
it seems to me that the first place

508
00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:39.690
that we want to kind of enter into here

509
00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:41.701
is the difference between Germ Theory

