00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:14.670
and I just want to sure emphasize that both of these things

522
00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.890
are important, and it's common sense,

523
00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:18.810
not just--
It's not a hard thing.

524
00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:20.503
I don't why for people insist on

525
00:22:20.503 --> 00:22:22.290
it being either, or.

526
00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:26.792
I mean, if you're an 85 year old with heart disease,

527
00:22:26.792 --> 00:22:28.470
who's a smoker, and sedentary,

528
00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.793
and overweight, and diabetic, and hypertensive,

529
00:22:30.793 --> 00:22:32.100
and you have a heart attack,

530
00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:35.075
you're gonna do worse than somebody who's 35,

531
00:22:35.075 --> 00:22:37.050
and, you know, an ultra runner

532
00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:38.670
who eats really healthily, et cetera.

533
00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.589
So, host health matters for cancer, and infection,

534
00:22:41.589 --> 00:22:44.280
and viral illnesses, and everything else,

535
00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:46.560
and that's the goal, to be the healthiest host we can.

536
00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.290
So, to get back to my two favorite topics

537
00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.750
of Germ Theory and Terrain Theory.

538
00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:55.050
Germ Theory was really popularized by Louis Pasteur,

539
00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:56.550
and Germ Theory basically says

540
00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:00.290
that a bad bug gets into your body, and it makes you sick,

541
00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:02.040
and that's absolutely true,

542
00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:03.660
and we see evidence of that all the time.

543
00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:06.320
We're seeing evidence of it now with SARS-CoV-2,

544
00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:12.090
but Terrain Theory says that a healthy host

545
00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:16.440
can manage illness and recover, and that's also true.

546
00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:17.940
So, we wash our hands.

547
00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.890
If you look at medical practices, you know,

548
00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:21.609
we wash our hands and we sanitize things

549
00:23:21.609 --> 00:23:23.530
because of Germ Theory,

550
00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.923
but we also eat a healthy diet, and exercise,

551
00:23:25.923 --> 00:23:28.320
and do all of those things because of Terrain Theory,

552
00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:29.850
because we want to be healthy hosts,

553
00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.850
and so, the two things are really, you know,

554
00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:34.020
again, they're hand in glove,

555
00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.410
they're not contrary to each other, and, you know,

556
00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:37.710
in the scientific community,

557
00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:39.300
there was all this back and forth,

558
00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:42.016
but, apparently, Antoine Bechamp,

559
00:23:42.016 --> 00:23:43.590
who popularized Terrain Theory,

560
00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:46.263
who's also a Frenchman like Louis Pasteur,

561
00:23:47.803 --> 00:23:49.620
folks, in the Bechamp camp,

562
00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:51.360
and I don't think they're two distinct camps,

563
00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.580
and they shouldn't be, but they sort of are.

564
00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:57.247
Apparently, Louis Pasteur, reportedly on his death bed

565
00:23:57.247 --> 00:24:01.560
said Bechamp was right, Terrain Theory. (laughs)

566
00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:02.400
I don't know if that...

567
00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.790
That might be a little dramatized,

568
00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:07.830
but clearly both things are really important.

569
00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:12.690
There are germs that get into our body, that can do us harm,

570
00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:16.980
but our health as hosts matters greatly in this equation,

571
00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:18.990
and it's also important to realize

572
00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:20.220
that not all germs are bad,

573
00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.170
and a scorched earth approach,

574
00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.200
where we, you know, go out and basically try

575
00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:26.250
and, you know, seek and destroy mission

576
00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:30.040
for all bacteria or viruses can lead us to some bad places.

577
00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:33.330
Right, once again, it's not a binary dualistic thing.

578
00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:34.470
It's not one or the other.

579
00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:35.910
These are both important.

580
00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:37.890
It does feel like,

581
00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:42.300
in the messaging or conversation around COVID,

582
00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.580
it was pretty much all about Germ Theory

583
00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:48.960
and maybe not enough about Terrain Theory.

584
00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.310
We can quibble about that,

585
00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:52.567
but it is interesting to consider

586
00:24:52.567 --> 00:24:55.290
the interplay between these two, you know,

587
00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:56.640
theories that are at play

588
00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.380
in terms of our susceptibility

589
00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:01.380
to a viral infection and disease,

590
00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:03.330
and what was amazing in the book

591
00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:08.330
was the statistics around people who were not healthy hosts,

592
00:25:08.372 --> 00:25:11.010
or who had dysbiosis,

593
00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.782
or some kind of dysregulated gut microbiome,

594
00:25:13.782 --> 00:25:15.930
and how they ended up faring

595
00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:17.910
when they came into contact with the virus.

596
00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.360
Yeah, it's an incredibly predictive marker,

597
00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.020
the health of the microbiome.

598
00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:25.020
So, one of the studies that I talk about in the book is

599
00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:26.865
a study that looked at what's going on

600
00:25:26.865 --> 00:25:29.160
in your microbiome as a predictor

601
00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:31.949
of acute respiratory illness, and even death,

602
00:25:31.949 --> 00:25:35.633
and the accuracy of identifying these different organisms,

603
00:25:35.633 --> 00:25:39.750
and the one in particular, faecalibacterium prausnitzii,

604
00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.800
that's really this incredible bacterium

605
00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:45.780
that's associated with eating a lot of plant fiber,

606
00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:47.220
and protective for other reasons

607
00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:50.220
in terms of short chain fatty acids and immune regulation.

608
00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.474
High levels of faecalibacterium prausnitzii

609
00:25:52.474 --> 00:25:56.457
were very predictive of a good outcome,

610
00:25:56.457 --> 00:25:58.380
and low levels, the opposite,

611
00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:01.900
and the accuracy in this study was 92%,

612
00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:04.380
which is much higher than looking at age,

613
00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:07.509
gender, comorbidity, or even inflammatory markers

614
00:26:07.509 --> 00:26:09.990
like the C-reactive protein, et cetera.

615
00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:12.768
So, they were seeing really incredible correlation

616
00:26:12.768 --> 00:26:16.281
between this marker of a healthy microbiome

617
00:26:16.281 --> 00:26:18.600
and outcome from COVID,

618
00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:20.910
and that's true for other viral illnesses also.

619
00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:24.140
We've seen similar statistics for influenza, for rotavirus,

620
00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:28.440
et cetera, and again, we shouldn't be surprised by that,

621
00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.060
but somehow we are,

622
00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.250
When I would watch reports on television,

623
00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:35.490
and I think we'll look back at this time

624
00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:38.070
and see a lot of things we didn't do right,

625
00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:40.200
and one of that was the television reporting,

626
00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:41.430
which was really designed

627
00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:44.220
to just completely freak us out and terrorize us,

628
00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.440
make us scared, and I would look at it

629
00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.600
with my family, and would say,

630
00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:53.600
you know, I bet you this person is taking an acid blocker,

631
00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.740
has obesity, is, you know,

632
00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:59.730
maybe on an immuno compromising medication, et cetera.

633
00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:04.260
So, again, there is predictability to this,

634
00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:06.660
not 100% of the time, of course,

635
00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.060
but there are patterns that we see

636
00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:11.370
when we look at some of these factors,

637
00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:15.180
host factors that are the primary determinants of outcome.

638
00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:16.795
It's not the virulence of the virus.

639
00:27:16.795 --> 00:27:19.291
When we take a population of people

640
00:27:19.291 --> 00:27:21.995
who are exposed to the same virus

641
00:27:21.995 --> 00:27:23.335
and we look at different outcomes,

642
00:27:23.335 --> 00:27:26.070
those outcomes are all exposed to the same virus,

643
00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.822
those outcomes are very much dependent

644
00:27:28.822 --> 00:27:31.140
on what the individual host factors are,

645
00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:33.360
and many of those are things that we can mitigate,

646
00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:34.440
we can do things about.

647
00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:38.135
Yeah, I mean 92% is quite remarkable, right?

648
00:27:38.135 --> 00:27:39.555
It's pretty damn good.
And that's without

649
00:27:39.555 --> 00:27:42.810
considering any other factors.

650
00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:45.415
When you actually then fold in, you know,

651
00:27:45.415 --> 00:27:49.583
some comorbidities, I think the statistic I saw is

652
00:27:49.583 --> 00:27:52.060
that it goes up to, like 98%, predictability.

653
00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:53.190
93%.

654
00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:54.023
Is that what it is?
Yeah.

655
00:27:54.023 --> 00:27:56.395
Yeah, you get an extra percentage point.

656
00:27:56.395 --> 00:27:58.775
So, in other words, we all know people,

657
00:27:58.775 --> 00:28:00.822
or just, you know, the general case of,

658
00:28:00.822 --> 00:28:03.487
like, "Well, this person, you know,

659
00:28:03.487 --> 00:28:05.579
"ended up in the ICU," it doesn't have to be COVID,

660
00:28:05.579 --> 00:28:07.080
whatever disease, right?

661
00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:10.470
This person got exposed, and they ended up violently ill,

662
00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:11.430
and this other person

663
00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:14.130
who was standing right next to that person

664
00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:16.890
didn't become symptomatic at all.

665
00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:17.723
Why is that?

666
00:28:17.723 --> 00:28:18.793
And we just, "Well, who knows?"

667
00:28:18.793 --> 00:28:21.740
Like, genetics, or we just sort of dismiss it,

668
00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:23.280
and what you're saying is,

669
00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:25.633
no, actually when you look at the gut,

670
00:28:25.633 --> 00:28:27.255
it becomes quite clear

671
00:28:27.255 --> 00:28:29.360
why some people fare better than others.

672
00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:30.240
Absolutely.

673
00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:31.465
And these other co-factors, obviously.

674
00:28:31.465 --> 00:28:33.630
And it's important for people to understand

675
00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:35.460
there's not blame being assigned here.

676
00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:37.380
We're not saying, you know,

677
00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:40.410
you got sick and ended up on a ventilator

678
00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:42.580
because you have obesity and that's your fault.

679
00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:44.970
The whole point of this is to recognize

680
00:28:44.970 --> 00:28:48.000
that these are factors that we can identify

681
00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:50.070
that can help us prognosticate,

682
00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:52.043
and more importantly, that we can remediate,

683
00:28:52.043 --> 00:28:54.750
ideally, so that this doesn't end up happening

684
00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:56.433
to you again, or you recover.

685
00:28:56.433 --> 00:29:00.270
So, you know, it's important for people to understand

686
00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:02.100
that this is not about blaming the victim.

687
00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.033
Somebody gets sick from COVID

688
00:29:03.033 --> 00:29:05.340
regardless of their circumstances.

689
00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.820
It's tragic, but it's really essential

690
00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.500
to point out and to identify,

691
00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:12.090
what are these factors?

692
00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:14.820
So that we can do risk mitigation

693
00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:17.010
and also maybe we can think about

694
00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:20.160
our strategies for how we approach our next pandemic.

695
00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:21.930
Who's at high risk and who's not,

696
00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:23.020
as opposed to just, you know,

697
00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:25.770
blanket recommendations.
Blanket policy to everybody.

698
00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:26.670
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

699
00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.140
Well, let's get into that.

700
00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:29.700
I mean, obviously,

701
00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:32.550
the key thing we want to avoid is dysbiosis.

702
00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:35.630
Dysbiosis is something that we've talked about extensively

703
00:29:35.630 --> 00:29:38.550
on the show with Dr. B, your peer,

704
00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:40.637
your colleague in this world.
Love him.

705
00:29:40.637 --> 00:29:41.553
Yeah.

706
00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:45.090
So, if you want to hear us go on for hours about that,

707
00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:46.500
you should listen to those episodes,

708
00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:49.320
but in terms of the causes,

709
00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:52.290
like, the primary causes of dysbiosis,

710
00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:53.610
I learned a lot reading your book

711
00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:56.223
and about certain things that I didn't know

712
00:29:56.223 --> 00:29:59.880
can be driving this, that are easily remediated.

713
00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:04.290
Like, let's start with PPIs, proton pump inhibitors.

714
00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.510
You already kind of referenced it a moment ago,

715
00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:08.220
but I was not aware of this.

716
00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:09.489
Yeah, you started with my favorite one

717
00:30:09.489 --> 00:30:10.980
and I'll tell you, Rich,

718
00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:14.736
a big part of the motivation for writing the book

719
00:30:14.736 --> 00:30:19.470
had to do with that pivotal study on PPIs,

720
00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.380
and I do use my husband as a little bit of a sounding board.

721
00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:24.101
So, I remember when that study came out

722
00:30:24.101 --> 00:30:26.299
in the summer of 2020, I said to him,

723
00:30:26.299 --> 00:30:28.320
because he's not in medicine, so he's,

724
00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:30.396
you know, I'm surrounded by a lot of medical colleagues

725
00:30:30.396 --> 00:30:32.820
and we all know a lot of the same stuff,

726
00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:34.777
but I said to him, I said,

727
00:30:34.777 --> 00:30:37.117
"You know that if you're on an acid blocker,

728
00:30:37.117 --> 00:30:39.770
"you're going to be much more likely to get COVID,"

729
00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.977
and he was like, "What?

730
00:30:41.977 --> 00:30:43.020
"Why?"

731
00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:44.043
And I said, "Because stomach acid..."

732
00:30:44.043 --> 00:30:45.450
And he was like...

733
00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:46.600
But I kept saying, "Well, you know that, right?"

734
00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:48.637
And he goes, "I don't know that.

735
00:30:48.637 --> 00:30:50.160
"How would I know that?"

736
00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.000
And then I realized,

737
00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:55.080
not only did he, in a non-medical field not know that,

738
00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:57.420
a lot of my medical colleagues didn't know that,

739
00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:00.030
a lot of my gastroenterology colleagues didn't know that.

740
00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.067
So, the study was a population based study

741
00:31:02.067 --> 00:31:04.509
of about 54,000 patients,

742
00:31:04.509 --> 00:31:08.430
and they looked at people taking a proton pump inhibitor.

743
00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:11.430
Those, you know, you might know, it as a little purple pill.

744
00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:14.400
So, that's drugs like Prilosec, Nexium, Aciphex,

745
00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:15.710
Protonix are a whole host of them on the market,

746
00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:19.740
and these drugs are--
Like an antacid, right?

747
00:31:19.740 --> 00:31:21.393
An antacid, technically, is a little bit different,

748
00:31:21.393 --> 00:31:22.650
shorter acting.

749
00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:25.380
The difference with the proton pump inhibitors is

750
00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:26.610
that they're long acting,

751
00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:27.600
and these drugs have been

752
00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:30.450
amongst the most commonly prescribed drugs in the world

753
00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:33.630
because they work amazingly well.

754
00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:37.017
They're an incredibly efficient class of drugs

755
00:31:37.017 --> 00:31:38.730
for doing what they're supposed to do,

756
00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:41.940
which is shutting down that proton potassium ATPase,

757
00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.340
that acid pump in your stomach.

758
00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:45.417
So, when you take these drugs,

759
00:31:45.417 --> 00:31:47.010
and it takes a couple days

760
00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:49.170
to get levels to the sufficient point

761
00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:51.390
where you essentially have no stomach acid,

762
00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:54.270
and so, for people who suffer from acid reflux,

763
00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:55.767
they love these drugs, because you take these drugs,

764
00:31:55.767 --> 00:31:57.840
and now you can do all the things you're not supposed to do.

765
00:31:57.840 --> 00:31:59.774
You can eat a big heavy meal late at night

766
00:31:59.774 --> 00:32:04.774
and you feel fine, but stomach acid is there for a reason.

767
00:32:05.667 --> 00:32:07.385
In addition to it being one of

768
00:32:07.385 --> 00:32:10.560
the most important components of digestion,

769
00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:14.670
we need stomach acid to provide the right acidic milieu

770
00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:16.410
for the digestive enzymes to work,

771
00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:17.910
so the food can be broken down

772
00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:21.330
to provide the right pH, so the nutrients can be assimilated

773
00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:24.060
and absorbed, and we've seen with these drugs,

774
00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:25.800
what happens when you take 'em long term.

775
00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:27.720
I'm not talking about taking this drug

776
00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:28.553
for a month or six weeks

777
00:32:28.553 --> 00:32:31.590
because you have an ulcer or you have bad reflux.

778
00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:32.967
I'm talking about people taking these drugs

779
00:32:32.967 --> 00:32:34.650
for several months, and this study,

780
00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:36.870
it was looking at people who are taking the drug

781
00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:38.299
for four months or more, and some people

782
00:32:38.299 --> 00:32:40.380
are on these drugs for decades.

783
00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:42.360
So, what the study found is

784
00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:45.540
that people taking one of these drugs once a day

785
00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:48.720
were twice as likely to have a positive test for COVID,

786
00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.730
and people taking the drugs twice a day,

787
00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:53.940
it was a three to four-fold increased risk,

788
00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:58.020
and that's because stomach acid denatures viral protein.

789
00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.230
So, when you're exposed to a lot of these viruses,

790
00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:01.920
the entry point for a lot of us,

791
00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:03.320
it can be our respiratory system,

792
00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:05.910
it can also be our digestive system,

793
00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:10.440
and in fact the ACE2 receptors that bind SARS-CoV-2,

794
00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:14.130
we've about 100 times more ACE2 receptors in our GI tract

795
00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:16.093
than we have in our lungs, and that's why GI symptoms

796
00:33:16.093 --> 00:33:18.870
have been so common with this virus.

797
00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:19.970
So, you're exposed to the virus,

798
00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:22.590
it gets in through your mouth, you swallow it.

799
00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:25.380
If you have adequate levels of stomach acid,

800
00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:27.570
the acid denatures a viral protein,

801
00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:29.550
and can prevent it from binding

802
00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.949
to those intestinal epithelial cells

803
00:33:31.949 --> 00:33:33.688
and infecting them, and creating

804
00:33:33.688 --> 00:33:35.940
that cascade of inflammation,

805
00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.460
but if you're now on a proton pump inhibitor,

806
00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:39.902
and if you've been on one for several months,

807
00:33:39.902 --> 00:33:43.350
and you essentially don't have any stomach acid,

808
00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:45.213
now you're defenseless, because now your stomach

809
00:33:45.213 --> 00:33:48.720
has been converted from a hostile,

810
00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:50.430
acidic environment for viruses,

811
00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:52.680
to a friendly, alkaline environment,

812
00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:54.998
and so, you know, I read that study,

813
00:33:54.998 --> 00:33:56.536
and I remembered talking

814
00:33:56.536 --> 00:33:59.580
to my literary agent, Howard Yoon, about it,

815
00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:01.177
and I was like, "Howard, you know,

816
00:34:01.177 --> 00:34:02.640
"people really don't know,"

817
00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:04.987
and he said, "Well, you know, maybe write an editorial.

818
00:34:04.987 --> 00:34:06.570
"You know, maybe an editorial or something,"

819
00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:07.597
and I was like, "Yeah, maybe I'll do that.

820
00:34:07.597 --> 00:34:10.350
"It'll be good to spread the word,"

821
00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:12.880
and then the microbiome study came out

822
00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:15.630
with the faecalibacterium prausnitzii,

823
00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.250
high levels of that being protective,

824
00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:20.107
and they saw the bacteria enterococcus faecalis,

825
00:34:20.107 --> 00:34:23.521
high levels of that being negatively predictive

826
00:34:23.521 --> 00:34:25.320
in the sense that those are associated

827
00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:26.880
with the worst outcome,

828
00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:30.780
and I just felt like nobody's talking about host health,

829
00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:32.100
and there's lots of people talking about

830
00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.500
vaccines and other important things,

831
00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:37.495
but this is an area that I really know a lot about,

832
00:34:37.495 --> 00:34:40.710
with the gut, and it just felt like

833
00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:42.540
that part of the public health message

834
00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.450
was missing and, Rich, in some ways even worse.

835
00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:48.120
It felt like if you talked about that,

836
00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:48.953
you were somehow saying

837
00:34:48.953 --> 00:34:51.510
this other stuff is important.
You're anti other thing.

838
00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:52.710
Which I just don't understand,

839
00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.418
because I'm very pro all the other things

840
00:34:54.418 --> 00:34:57.420
that we have in medicine to serve us,

841
00:34:57.420 --> 00:34:59.485
but making sure people understand that,

842
00:34:59.485 --> 00:35:02.430
and there are people who have to take these drugs,

843
00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:04.530
but these drugs are very over-prescribed.

844
00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:05.820
There have been studies suggesting

845
00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:08.080
that 70%, 80% of people taking them,

846
00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:10.950
particularly older people over 65,

847
00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:13.080
who are also at risk for other reasons

848
00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:17.280
that the majority of the prescriptions are unnecessary,

849
00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.020
and the interesting thing about PPI's,

850
00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:22.560
Rich, is we have seen for decades in the GI world,

851
00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:25.350
this increased risk of infection from PPI.

852
00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:28.440
So, if you look at something like Clostridium difficile,

853
00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:32.730
it's a bacteria that is often prevalent in hospitals,

854
00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:35.610
and we think about it as an antibiotic associated diarrhea,

855
00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:37.260
because you take antibiotics,

856
00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.660
you wipe out a lot of your healthy bacteria,

857
00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.445
and then the C. diff that's lurking,

858
00:35:41.445 --> 00:35:44.190
or that you've caught from, you know,

859
00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:45.963
a door, or something in the hospital

860
00:35:45.963 --> 00:35:47.821
now starts to proliferate,

861
00:35:47.821 --> 00:35:50.640
and lo and behold, you have this C. diff colitis,

862
00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:54.300
which, you know, kills about 30,000 people a year in the US.

863
00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:58.170
So, we've known for a long time that proton pump inhibitors

864
00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:00.330
are a major risk factor for C. diff.

865
00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:01.306
We know proton pump inhibitors

866
00:36:01.306 --> 00:36:03.744
are a major risk factor for campylobacter jejuni,

867
00:36:03.744 --> 00:36:06.150
a very common foodborne infection,

868
00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:10.350
and other enteric illnesses that affect the GI tract.

869
00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:12.090
So, in the GI world,

870
00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:14.190
it really shouldn't have been a surprise,

871
00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:16.770
but I think the numbers were still pretty astounding

872
00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.121
that it could increase the risk that much,

873
00:36:19.121 --> 00:36:22.770
and, you know, for people on PPIs,

874
00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:24.961
there are people who have serious indications,

875
00:36:24.961 --> 00:36:27.478
but, again, a lot of the people who are on PPIs

876
00:36:27.478 --> 00:36:30.636
can probably get off them, or take a lower dose,

877
00:36:30.636 --> 00:36:34.020
or use an antacid, something that you use sporadically.

878
00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:35.564
So, an antacid that you take

879
00:36:35.564 --> 00:36:37.620
only when you're having active reflux,

880
00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:40.440
as opposed to a PPI that you take every day,

881
00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:44.821
and, you know, having a really sort of frank conversation

882
00:36:44.821 --> 00:36:46.792
with your prescriber about

883
00:36:46.792 --> 00:36:48.539
what your indication is for this drug

884
00:36:48.539 --> 00:36:50.300
and whether you really need it,

885
00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:52.680
and then coming up with a plan for how to taper off,

886
00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:54.080
which you will find in the book.

887
00:36:54.080 --> 00:36:55.830
Yeah, I mean, there's a difference

888
00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:56.940
between somebody who has

889
00:36:56.940 --> 00:37:01.440
some kind of chronic acid reflux issue

890
00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.671
versus the individual who has a terrible diet

891
00:37:04.671 --> 00:37:09.120
and is creating that as a result of lifestyle choices,

892
00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:10.680
and wants to go on the PPI ,

893
00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:13.290
so they can continue to perpetuate the bad habit, right?

894
00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:16.488
So, it's driving multiple negative outcomes,

895
00:37:16.488 --> 00:37:20.040
because it's allowing you to continue to do something

896
00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.780
that your body is trying to tell you,

897
00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:23.390
you shouldn't be doing, right?

898
00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:26.790
Your stomach pH is what it is for a reason.

899
00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:29.670
It's not just so that it can optimally digest your food.

900
00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.400
It is this line of defense for these other pathogens,

901
00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:33.840
and when you change that pH,

902
00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:36.777
obviously, you are weakening your body's ability

903
00:37:36.777 --> 00:37:39.730
to do what it's naturally you know there to do.

904
00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.455
And that's why we see so many manifestations

905
00:37:43.455 --> 00:37:46.830
of long-term PPI use in different parts of the body.

906
00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:48.810
We see an increased risk for dementia,

907
00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:50.490
we see kidney disease,

908
00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:52.290
we see an increased fracture risk,

909
00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.678
because it's affecting and disrupting

910
00:37:54.678 --> 00:37:56.610
the delivery of nutrients

911
00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:58.530
to all these different parts of the body,

912
00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:00.200
but the marketing is so fascinating,

913
00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:03.960
and I remember reading this terrific book a long time ago,

914
00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:05.310
before I ever wrote a book,

915
00:38:05.310 --> 00:38:07.990
called "Our Daily Meds" by Melody Petersen.

916
00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:10.800
She's, I believe, a medical journalist

917
00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:12.250
maybe for the New York Times,

918
00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:15.000
and she described in that book

919
00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:18.450
the campaign around acid blocker drugs.

920
00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:21.630
So, I think so many people out there believe

921
00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:24.570
that they have overproduction of stomach acid.

922
00:38:24.570 --> 00:38:26.040
Overproduction of stomach acid

923
00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:27.466
is an exceedingly rare condition

924
00:38:27.466 --> 00:38:30.149
called Zollinger Ellison syndrome

925
00:38:30.149 --> 00:38:32.940
that occurs in about one in a million people in the US.

926
00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:34.771
So, that means there maybe 350,

927
00:38:34.771 --> 00:38:36.415
close to 400 people in the US

928
00:38:36.415 --> 00:38:40.410
who actually have overproduction of stomach acid.

929
00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:42.090
For everybody else who has reflux,

930
00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:44.605
what they have is inappropriate relaxation

931
00:38:44.605 --> 00:38:46.680
of that sphincter, that valve

932
00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:48.354
between the esophagus and the stomach

933
00:38:48.354 --> 00:38:50.490
that's supposed to shut tight

934
00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:52.370
to keep the stomach contents in the stomach.

935
00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:55.380
So, we have inappropriate relaxation opening

936
00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:58.383
and then the acid comes up, but it's not overproduction,

937
00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:02.370
and so, if you start to believe, you know,

938
00:39:02.370 --> 00:39:04.807
if the marketing is around overproduction, you think,

939
00:39:04.807 --> 00:39:06.870
"Oh, I have too much stomach acid,"

940
00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:09.265
and the marketing is around stomach acid being bad,

941
00:39:09.265 --> 00:39:11.887
then it's an easy step to then say, "Oh, yeah,

942
00:39:11.887 --> 00:39:14.580
"let me block my stomach acid, and I'll be better off,"

943
00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:15.840
but if you really understand

944
00:39:15.840 --> 00:39:18.780
that it's that inappropriate opening of the valve,

945
00:39:18.780 --> 00:39:21.087
and what opens it inappropriately?

946
00:39:21.087 --> 00:39:22.215
Eating late at night,

947
00:39:22.215 --> 00:39:24.660
after the stomach is basically shut down

948
00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:27.240
and gone to sleep, because it follows the circadian rhythm.

949
00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:29.663
So, eating a large meal late at night, overfilling it,

950
00:39:29.663 --> 00:39:32.370
the stomach's about the size of your fist,

951
00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.310
eating food with a high fat,

952
00:39:34.310 --> 00:39:36.603
high protein content, like a lot of animal protein,

953
00:39:36.603 --> 00:39:39.960
which will slow down emptying of the stomach,

954
00:39:39.960 --> 00:39:43.470
caffeine, alcohol, chocolate, all of that stuff.

955
00:39:43.470 --> 00:39:45.296
So, when you understand what's going on,

956
00:39:45.296 --> 00:39:48.262
and you think, "Okay, I can do some stuff here.

957
00:39:48.262 --> 00:39:50.047
"I can eat a little bit earlier,

958
00:39:50.047 --> 00:39:52.987
"I can eat a smaller meal, I can eat my big meal earlier,

959
00:39:52.987 --> 00:39:57.270
"I can cut down on my dairy, and alcohol, and caffeine."

960
00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:59.730
You start to understand how you can effect that,

961
00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:01.932
as opposed to doing this incredibly drastic

962
00:40:01.932 --> 00:40:04.193
and potentially disastrous thing,

963
00:40:04.193 --> 00:40:08.360
which is to completely shut down your acid pump.

964
