00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.060
Well, continuing on this,

999
00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:47.010
I suppose the lane that we're in right now

1000
00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:50.097
is the impact of pharmaceuticals on gut dysregulation

1001
00:41:50.097 --> 00:41:53.250
and, in turn, immune system dysfunction.

1002
00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:55.770
We're all fairly familiar, I think, at this point,

1003
00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:59.550
with the havoc that antibiotics can wreak on this system.

1004
00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:01.890
So, I don't know that we need to explore that any further.

1005
00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:04.020
It's sort of self-evident,

1006
00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.130
but there's some other things here

1007
00:42:05.130 --> 00:42:06.000
that you talk about in the book

1008
00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:09.063
that I also didn't fully appreciate,

1009
00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:12.120
one of them being NSAIDs.

1010
00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:15.420
Basically, you know, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs,

1011
00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:18.200
the Motrins and the Advils.

1012
00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:19.760
Yeah, yeah, so--

1013
00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:20.940
Because I've always thought of those

1014
00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:22.290
being relatively benign.

1015
00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:24.430
Well, as an athlete, you've probably relied

1016
00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:26.430
on them a lot too, right?

1017
00:42:26.430 --> 00:42:30.153
And I'll just tell you my little story about these drugs.

1018
00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:32.333
As a gastroenterologist,

1019
00:42:32.333 --> 00:42:34.990
when I used to take calls the hospital,

1020
00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:37.320
pretty much at least once a month,

1021
00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.990
we would see somebody with near fatal bleeding

1022
00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:43.109
from taking a nonsteroidal one too many doses,

1023
00:42:43.109 --> 00:42:44.550
and you don't need a lot,

1024
00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:46.230
unlike Tylenol, acetaminophen,

1025
00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:48.160
where to get liver injury from Tylenol,

1026
00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:51.269
there's a set dose amount that you have to exceed,

1027
00:42:51.269 --> 00:42:52.459
NSAIDs aren't as forgiving.

1028
00:42:52.459 --> 00:42:55.860
So, you can take, you know, 800 milligrams of Motrin,

1029
00:42:55.860 --> 00:42:58.380
which isn't that much, and end up with a huge ulcer.

1030
00:42:58.380 --> 00:42:59.850
If the area that ulcerated

1031
00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.520
happened to be over a major blood vessel,

1032
00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:04.560
you can potentially bleed to death,

1033
00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:06.660
and so, literally at least once a month,

1034
00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:09.030
we would see somebody with massive, you know,

1035
00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:12.540
nerve fatal bleeding from an ulcer in the wrong spot

1036
00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:17.540
that was created by NSAIDs, and so, as a result of that,

1037
00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:19.770
I'm very wary of taking those drugs.

1038
00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:21.510
The only time I take 'em,

1039
00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:24.435
I usually would take 'em at around mile 20 of a marathon.

1040
00:43:24.435 --> 00:43:26.190
Yeah. (laughs)
I'm a slow,

1041
00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:27.360
but steady marathoner,

1042
00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:28.860
but the knees aren't what they used to be.

1043
00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:30.920
So, usually around mile 20 when it just, you know,

1044
00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:33.390
feels like bone grading on bone.

1045
00:43:33.390 --> 00:43:37.091
I would take some, and typically at the end of the race,

1046
00:43:37.091 --> 00:43:39.307
I would be vomiting and, you know, people are like,

1047
00:43:39.307 --> 00:43:40.800
"Oh, you really ran hard."

1048
00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:43.230
I'm like, "No, it's the NSAID."

1049
00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:46.320
They really affect me, so I get really nauseated,

1050
00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:49.830
but again, like the PPIs, these drugs work really well.

1051
00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:54.213
In 2017, I tore my MCL snowboarding.

1052
00:43:55.105 --> 00:43:57.110
I was out in Utah, couldn't straighten the leg,

1053
00:43:57.110 --> 00:44:00.500
was in a lot of pain, difficult to fly back,

1054
00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:02.587
and my husband was like, "Just take some of this.

1055
00:44:02.587 --> 00:44:03.840
"Like, why are you in pain?"

1056
00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:05.670
It took away the pain.

1057
00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:06.719
I mean, it was like magic,

1058
00:44:06.719 --> 00:44:08.430
and maybe I shouldn't be surprised.

1059
00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:10.810
I'm a doctor, but I generally don't take these drugs.

1060
00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:14.338
It was incredible how well this drug worked

1061
00:44:14.338 --> 00:44:15.171
to take away the pain.

1062
00:44:15.171 --> 00:44:18.540
So, you can understand for people who have chronic injuries,

1063
00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:22.050
who are in chronic pain, who have inflammatory conditions,

1064
00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:24.113
it's understandable how you can take this drug

1065
00:44:24.113 --> 00:44:25.920
and become dependent on it,

1066
00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.434
but it's really important to understand,

1067
00:44:27.434 --> 00:44:30.240
what's the price you're paying on the flip side?

1068
00:44:30.240 --> 00:44:32.860
So, we talked about those ulcers,

1069
00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:36.120
NSAIDs make tiny little holes in your gut lining

1070
00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:39.330
that can sometimes be bigger holes that we call erosions,

1071
00:44:39.330 --> 00:44:42.000
or can be really big holes that we call ulcers.

1072
00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:45.660
So, that's what those are, and when we do endoscopy,

1073
00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:47.446
or a technique called a video capsule endoscopy,

1074
00:44:47.446 --> 00:44:49.260
a little pill cam that you swallow

1075
00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:51.450
that takes pictures through the GI tract.

1076
00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:54.660
When we do that in asymptomatic people taking NSAIDs,

1077
00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:56.340
we see a very high percentage rate.

1078
00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:57.510
We see in some studies

1079
00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:59.988
up to 30% of people having these erosions,

1080
00:44:59.988 --> 00:45:02.020
these little holes in the gut,

1081
00:45:02.020 --> 00:45:04.338
and so, it's important for people to also understand

1082
00:45:04.338 --> 00:45:06.843
that it's not just if you're symptomatic.

1083
00:45:06.843 --> 00:45:08.545
Like me, you're nauseated, you're vomiting

1084
00:45:08.545 --> 00:45:12.000
because you took too much Motrin, and you feel really sick.

1085
00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:15.330
You can be asymptomatic and still have this,

1086
00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:17.020
and again, there are people who need to take these drugs.

1087
00:45:17.020 --> 00:45:19.050
There are people with severe arthritis,

1088
00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:21.150
where these drugs are the only thing

1089
00:45:21.150 --> 00:45:22.274
that keep them functional,

1090
00:45:22.274 --> 00:45:25.050
so that they can move without pain,

1091
00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:27.750
but there is definitely a flip side to that,

1092
00:45:27.750 --> 00:45:31.890
and so, the integrity of the gut lining is really, again,

1093
00:45:31.890 --> 00:45:33.070
we talked about stomach acid,

1094
00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:35.430
the gut lining is another one of those really,

1095
00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:36.841
really important barriers.

1096
00:45:36.841 --> 00:45:39.288
It is physically, literally a barrier

1097
00:45:39.288 --> 00:45:41.940
to keep the virus from getting in

1098
00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:43.920
and penetrating, getting into the bloodstream,

1099
00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:45.420
traveling to different parts of the body,

1100
00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:47.070
wreaking havoc in the body,

1101
00:45:47.070 --> 00:45:49.230
and what we see is

1102
00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:52.380
that these NSAIDs end up creating breaches,

1103
00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:54.502
increasing the intestinal permeability,

1104
00:45:54.502 --> 00:45:56.370
and making you more susceptible.

1105
00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:58.280
Right, so this is leaky gut.

1106
00:45:58.280 --> 00:45:59.940
This is essentially--
Or a version of leaky gut.

1107
00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:03.120
You know, leaky gut is one of those, again,

1108
00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:07.450
ephemeral, ethereal terms that, you know,

1109
00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:10.950
people understand a lot of different things by it,

1110
00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:13.470
but I remind people that what we're talking about

1111
00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:14.610
when we say leaky gut is

1112
00:46:14.610 --> 00:46:16.540
an increase in intestinal permeability,

1113
00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:18.780
and that's a mechanism

1114
00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:21.420
more than necessarily an actual disease.

1115
00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:23.598
I see.
It is a mechanism,

1116
00:46:23.598 --> 00:46:24.930
and other things can do it.

1117
00:46:24.930 --> 00:46:25.763
You know, intense exercise

1118
00:46:25.763 --> 00:46:29.000
can increase intestinal permeability temporarily.

1119
00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:31.830
I mean, it would be an interesting study for you

1120
00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:34.080
after a really long run or bike ride

1121
00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:36.300
to check your intestinal permeability before and after.

1122
00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:37.740
There's some tests you can do and see,

1123
00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:39.000
but it comes back to normal.

1124
00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:40.980
So, that's a physiological response,

1125
00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.510
but what we're talking about,

1126
00:46:42.510 --> 00:46:44.250
and, I think, what's generally understood

1127
00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:45.810
when people say leaky gut

1128
00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:49.078
is a persistent increase in the permeability

1129
00:46:49.078 --> 00:46:51.600
that allows things that normally shouldn't,

1130
00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:53.464
to go through that gut lining,

1131
00:46:53.464 --> 00:46:55.314
and enter the body, and be disseminated

1132
00:46:55.314 --> 00:46:57.930
to different parts of the body, and cause responses.

1133
00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:01.050
Right, so, in other words, viruses

1134
00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:04.380
can then leak through that membrane

1135
00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:08.176
and find their way into your tissues, and create disease.

1136
00:47:08.176 --> 00:47:09.270
Absolutely.

1137
00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:13.590
So, that's the relationship between the the gut issue

1138
00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:15.435
and the immune system issue.

1139
00:47:15.435 --> 00:47:17.493
That's it.
On that level, yeah.

1140
00:47:18.360 --> 00:47:20.700
What about birth control pills

1141
00:47:20.700 --> 00:47:23.040
and other forms of hormone therapy?

1142
00:47:23.040 --> 00:47:24.450
Yeah, hormonal therapy

1143
00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:25.687
can affect the balance of gut bacteria.

1144
00:47:25.687 --> 00:47:27.589
You know, there was a study done,

1145
00:47:27.589 --> 00:47:30.595
published in "the Journal of Nature" a few years ago,

1146
00:47:30.595 --> 00:47:33.810
and as you said, we all know about antibiotics,

1147
00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:35.460
and most of us are pretty familiar

1148
00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:39.660
with the damage that proton pump inhibitors do,

1149
00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:41.305
and that change in acid also changes

1150
00:47:41.305 --> 00:47:43.320
a gradient of gut bacteria.

1151
00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:44.490
It interrupts that,

1152
00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:47.990
and so, long-term PPIs can have the same effect,

1153
00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:51.390
if not worse, than antibiotics on dysbiosis,

1154
00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:53.253
but this article in nature,

1155
00:47:53.253 --> 00:47:56.580
looked at about 42 different classes of drugs,

1156
00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:59.050
and there was a wide variety of drugs

1157
00:47:59.050 --> 00:48:00.970
that are causing dysbiosis.

1158
00:48:00.970 --> 00:48:05.970
So, for example, the SSRIs, antidepressants that people use

1159
00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:09.570
can be associated drugs like Prozac.

1160
00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:12.930
There's associations with Prozac causing resistant E. coli.

1161
00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:16.680
I think about all the young women I see with, you know,

1162
00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:18.510
sort of recurrent urinary tract infections

1163
00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:21.030
who may be taking fluoxetine, Prozac.

1164
00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:23.483
So, there are all these drug interactions,

1165
00:48:23.483 --> 00:48:25.590
and hormonal therapy is another one,

1166
00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:28.560
and again, I'm not suggesting that these drugs are bad,

1167
00:48:28.560 --> 00:48:30.300
I am thrilled that we have

1168
00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:32.670
all of these medications in our arsenal,

1169
00:48:32.670 --> 00:48:35.550
but I'm a really firm believer

1170
00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:37.920
and a strong advocate for more judicious use,

1171
00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:40.560
and for people really understanding the risks,

1172
00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:41.610
that nothing is free.

1173
00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:43.380
There is always a flip side

1174
00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:45.360
with a pharmaceutical intervention,

1175
00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:47.280
and they need to understand the risk

1176
00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:49.398
and really personalize that based on

1177
00:48:49.398 --> 00:48:51.090
what their risk factors are.

1178
00:48:51.090 --> 00:48:54.420
So, for hormonal therapy, if you are somebody, for example,

1179
00:48:54.420 --> 00:48:58.170
with a strong family history, if you're a menopausal woman,

1180
00:48:58.170 --> 00:48:59.970
and you have a strong family history

1181
00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:03.180
or personal history of breast cancer or endometrial cancer,

1182
00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:04.680
you might consider that differently

1183
00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:07.350
versus if you're somebody with a history of heart disease,

1184
00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:10.290
and what goes into that, but the idea that, you know,

1185
00:49:10.290 --> 00:49:12.240
every postmenopausal woman should be

1186
00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:14.044
on hormonal replacement therapy,

1187
00:49:14.044 --> 00:49:15.310
or every young woman should be on birth control,

1188
00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:17.370
and, you know, the birth control is

1189
00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:18.630
one I really struggle with

1190
00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:22.230
because we know that unwanted pregnancies is

1191
00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:25.530
something that keeps women, and particularly young women,

1192
00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:28.840
and young women of color in poverty,

1193
00:49:28.840 --> 00:49:32.135
and so, birth control has revolutionized

1194
00:49:32.135 --> 00:49:35.970
the ability of young women to have control over their lives

1195
00:49:35.970 --> 00:49:38.507
and prevent unwanted pregnancies, and so on,

1196
00:49:38.507 --> 00:49:41.280
but at the same time, it's important to acknowledge

1197
00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:43.440
that there is a downside for some people,

1198
00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:44.957
and that it does potentially

1199
00:49:44.957 --> 00:49:48.150
have an effect on the microbiome,

1200
00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:50.205
not so much birth control just on its own,

1201
00:49:50.205 --> 00:49:51.990
but then if you add in,

1202
00:49:51.990 --> 00:49:54.450
this is somebody who's taken a lot of antibiotics,

1203
00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:56.040
this is somebody who's been on steroids,

1204
00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:58.785
this is somebody who's eaten a highly processed diet.

1205
00:49:58.785 --> 00:50:03.335
You know, it's one of those factors to take into account

1206
00:50:03.335 --> 00:50:04.980
as opposed to just, you know,

1207
00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:06.960
nobody should be on it or everybody should be on it.

1208
00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:08.698
So, this more personalized approach,

1209
00:50:08.698 --> 00:50:12.000
and understanding your own medical history

1210
00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:14.940
and risk stratification, and then putting that

1211
00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:17.670
in the context of the pharmaceutical is really important.

1212
00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:18.503
Yeah, beautifully put.

1213
00:50:18.503 --> 00:50:20.460
I mean, this is certainly not...

1214
00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:23.700
My intention is not to villainize any medication,

1215
00:50:23.700 --> 00:50:25.950
but I think it is so important

1216
00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:27.807
to understand the comprehensive nature

1217
00:50:27.807 --> 00:50:30.480
of what's going on when you ingest certain things.

1218
00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:32.621
I think that we have this limited,

1219
00:50:32.621 --> 00:50:34.980
or, you know, back to this idea

1220
00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:37.370
of being overly dualistic about things.

1221
00:50:37.370 --> 00:50:40.320
Like, I'm taking this thing for this single purpose,

1222
00:50:40.320 --> 00:50:45.320
but there's a cascade of ramifications to that,

1223
00:50:45.420 --> 00:50:47.100
and not the least of which is

1224
00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:48.870
how it's impacting our microbiome,

1225
00:50:48.870 --> 00:50:50.400
and I think it's, you know,

1226
00:50:50.400 --> 00:50:52.529
I can't say that when I take a medication,

1227
00:50:52.529 --> 00:50:56.490
I think, "What is this doing in my microbiome?"

1228
00:50:56.490 --> 00:50:57.323
Like, that's not the first thing--

1229
00:50:57.323 --> 00:50:58.410
You don't?
That comes to...

1230
00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:00.270
Well, I will now.

1231
00:51:00.270 --> 00:51:01.110
I should, you know.

1232
00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:03.635
I don't really take medications anyway,

1233
00:51:03.635 --> 00:51:06.963
unless I'm dealing with something acute,

1234
00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:10.410
but to understand that we have to think about

1235
00:51:10.410 --> 00:51:14.797
these things from a holistic perspective that,

1236
00:51:14.797 --> 00:51:16.599
you know, it's not just about, like,

1237
00:51:16.599 --> 00:51:19.427
you see the ad for the pharmaceutical on TV

1238
00:51:19.427 --> 00:51:24.427
and they rapid fire through all the terrible side effects,

1239
00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:27.630
but I don't know that I've ever heard anyone say,

1240
00:51:27.630 --> 00:51:31.320
you know, "Might lead to microbiome dysbiosis,"

1241
00:51:31.320 --> 00:51:32.520
or anything like that.
It should, right?

1242
00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:33.390
Don't you think?
Yeah.

1243
00:51:33.390 --> 00:51:36.106
I mean, a perfect example of that is cough syrup,

1244
00:51:36.106 --> 00:51:37.920
and that was one, I have to say

1245
00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:39.390
the mucus section in the book.

1246
00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:40.487
Yeah, let's talk about mucus.

1247
00:51:40.487 --> 00:51:43.935
Geeked out on, because I had been talking about

1248
00:51:43.935 --> 00:51:47.370
gut bacteria, and dysbiosis, and acid blockers

1249
00:51:47.370 --> 00:51:51.690
for a long time, but mucus was sort of a new area for me,

1250
00:51:51.690 --> 00:51:53.677
and a lot of people think of mucus as something that,

1251
00:51:53.677 --> 00:51:56.460
you know, it comes from your head or your lungs,

1252
00:51:56.460 --> 00:51:58.470
but the majority of mucus is made in your gut.

1253
00:51:58.470 --> 00:52:01.420
We make about one and a half liters of mucus a day,

1254
00:52:01.420 --> 00:52:04.230
and mucus serves some really important purposes.

1255
00:52:04.230 --> 00:52:05.970
So, it lubricates the gut,

1256
00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:08.610
obviously, as the products of digestion come through.

1257
00:52:08.610 --> 00:52:10.260
It provides a protective layer

1258
00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:13.830
for that razor thin intestinal lining I talked about,

1259
00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:15.480
and in the stomach,

1260
00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:17.730
it helps the stomach from being auto digested

1261
00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:19.260
by all the acid that's being produced.

1262
00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:20.880
So, mucus is really important,

1263
00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:24.150
but the role is much more than just a lubricant.

1264
00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:25.740
It's this cross.

1265
00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:27.470
Somebody, I forget who it was,

1266
00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:31.920
described it as a cross between jello and glue,

1267
00:52:31.920 --> 00:52:35.220
and it's a sticky matrix that ensnares things,

1268
00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:36.120
and then the celia,

1269
00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:38.010
the little finger light projections in the lungs,

1270
00:52:38.010 --> 00:52:41.490
move it all up, and you cough it out or you swallow it,

1271
00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:42.910
and if you swallow it, and you have stomach acid,

1272
00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:45.426
then the stomach acid denatures the protein

1273
00:52:45.426 --> 00:52:46.983
and does its thing.

1274
00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:51.855
If you suppress mucus, you know, you have a runny nose.

1275
00:52:51.855 --> 00:52:53.730
When you have a runny nose,

1276
00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:55.890
it's because your body is producing more mucus

1277
00:52:55.890 --> 00:52:57.540
to fight something that you're dealing with,

1278
00:52:57.540 --> 00:53:00.180
whether that's a seasonal allergy or an infection

1279
00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:02.850
because mucus traps pollen, and irritants,

1280
00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:05.950
and smoke, and other things too, to expel it from your body.

1281
00:53:05.950 --> 00:53:09.297
So, you have a runny nose, you take a cough suppressant,

1282
00:53:09.297 --> 00:53:11.520
or some sort of antihistamine, or something

1283
00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:14.280
to dry up your mucus,

1284
00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:16.470
and now one of your main defense,

1285
00:53:16.470 --> 00:53:19.620
the ability of the mucus to trap and expel, is gone,

1286
00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:21.315
and so, now it's gonna stick around

1287
00:53:21.315 --> 00:53:23.190
and maybe get down into your lungs,

1288
00:53:23.190 --> 00:53:26.220
and what's really incredible, speaking of pharmaceuticals,

1289
00:53:26.220 --> 00:53:29.979
you look back at things like Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup

1290
00:53:29.979 --> 00:53:34.110
and the original Bayer formulation for cough syrup.

1291
00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:36.360
This stuff had heroin and morphine in it,

1292
00:53:36.360 --> 00:53:39.245
so it really made your kids stop coughing.

1293
00:53:39.245 --> 00:53:40.830
Maybe they didn't wake up.

1294
00:53:40.830 --> 00:53:41.663
I mean, it was incredible,

1295
00:53:41.663 --> 00:53:43.950
and really, for some of these ingredients,

1296
00:53:43.950 --> 00:53:45.453
it wasn't until the 1970s

1297
00:53:45.453 --> 00:53:47.385
that some of this stuff was taken out,

1298
00:53:47.385 --> 00:53:51.389
and so, the American Academy of Pediatrics now recommends

1299
00:53:51.389 --> 00:53:54.240
that you don't use these cough expectorants

1300
00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:56.370
or anti-expectorants in kids,

1301
00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:59.220
but they shouldn't be used in adults either, and again,

1302
00:53:59.220 --> 00:54:01.440
like, you have a hacking cough, you're trying to get relief,

1303
00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:04.440
you're, you know, keeping your partner up,

1304
00:54:04.440 --> 00:54:06.120
but again, it comes at a cost.

1305
00:54:06.120 --> 00:54:08.229
So, what are some other things that you can do

1306
00:54:08.229 --> 00:54:11.370
to help with this, as opposed

1307
00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:14.815
to blocking this really important host defense?

1308
00:54:14.815 --> 00:54:18.060
And the mucus also provides a layer.

1309
00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:20.130
You know, in the book I talk about,

1310
00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:21.510
I don't know anything about football,

1311
00:54:21.510 --> 00:54:22.480
but I used a football analogy.

1312
00:54:22.480 --> 00:54:23.770
It's the equivalent of, like,

1313
00:54:23.770 --> 00:54:26.830
going through 150 football fields to make a touchdown,

1314
00:54:26.830 --> 00:54:29.280
or something, just for the distance.

1315
00:54:29.280 --> 00:54:32.910
When you add that mucus layer on top of the lining,

1316
00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:34.978
it creates this protective layer that the viruses

1317
00:54:34.978 --> 00:54:38.070
have to wade through to penetrate the gut lining.

1318
00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:41.400
So, it serves these really important purposes,

1319
00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:45.420
and then there's also the whole concept of mucins in mucus,

1320
00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:49.500
which are the proteins in mucus that degrade enzymes.

1321
00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:52.860
So, just like stomach acid can denature viral protein,

1322
00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:57.000
mucin's in mucus, an enzyme that can degrade protein,

1323
00:54:57.000 --> 00:54:59.770
and depending on the composition of your mucins

1324
00:54:59.770 --> 00:55:02.295
and how healthy they are,

1325
00:55:02.295 --> 00:55:04.947
you can be the kind of person where, the virus gets in,

1326
00:55:04.947 --> 00:55:08.340
the mucins in your mucus immediately degrade it,

1327
00:55:08.340 --> 00:55:12.000
and you're good, versus your mucus isn't that good,

1328
00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:14.154
and it sticks around, and this has a lot to do

1329
00:55:14.154 --> 00:55:17.220
with the whole concept of super spreaders.

1330
00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:18.989
So, we know that for a lot of these viral illnesses,

1331
00:55:18.989 --> 00:55:22.110
for measles, for Ebola, for SARS-CoV-2,

1332
00:55:22.110 --> 00:55:23.989
it's often a small group of people

1333
00:55:23.989 --> 00:55:27.390
who are disseminating the virus widely,

1334
00:55:27.390 --> 00:55:30.930
and so, if you connect super spreading to mucus,

1335
00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:32.700
if you happen to get coughed on

1336
00:55:32.700 --> 00:55:35.040
by somebody who has SARS-CoV-2,

1337
00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:38.580
but who has really good mucus that has broken down,

1338
00:55:38.580 --> 00:55:41.180
degraded the virus, you're not gonna get infected,

1339
00:55:41.180 --> 00:55:42.450
but if you get coughed on

1340
00:55:42.450 --> 00:55:45.755
by somebody whose mucus is not so hot,

1341
00:55:45.755 --> 00:55:48.570
they're more likely to spread it to you.

1342
00:55:48.570 --> 00:55:51.870
So, you have super spreaders and you have super recipients

1343
00:55:51.870 --> 00:55:55.080
based on, you know, if you have really healthy mucus,

1344
00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:56.610
you're gonna be protected, versus not.

1345
00:55:56.610 --> 00:55:58.283
So, the cool thing to me is, like,

1346
00:55:58.283 --> 00:56:00.360
this is all stuff we can work on.

1347
00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:03.670
We can hydrate to improve the quality of our mucus.

1348
00:56:03.670 --> 00:56:06.150
We can not smoke if we're smokers.

1349
00:56:06.150 --> 00:56:08.490
We can think about the quality of our external environment.

1350
00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:11.040
There are lots of things that go into it,

1351
00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:14.500
and a really fascinating aside about mucus is

1352
00:56:14.500 --> 00:56:17.790
with pregnancy, the cervical mucus,

1353
00:56:17.790 --> 00:56:21.960
the thickness of it tells us a lot about what's going on.

1354
00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:25.612
So, that's something that obstetricians have done for years.

1355
00:56:25.612 --> 00:56:28.216
During labor, they will examine,

1356
00:56:28.216 --> 00:56:30.060
or sort of leading up to labor,

1357
00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:32.527
they'll do a manual exam, and they'll tell the thickness,

1358
00:56:32.527 --> 00:56:35.280
and they can feel it with their hands,

1359
00:56:35.280 --> 00:56:38.520
and if the mucus is really thin and watery,

1360
00:56:38.520 --> 00:56:42.530
they know that that person is at risk for preterm infection

1361
00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:45.250
because the mucus isn't thick enough

1362
00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:47.584
to prevent pathogens from getting in,

1363
00:56:47.584 --> 00:56:48.885
and they may do something differently.

1364
00:56:48.885 --> 00:56:52.870
So, you know, it's mucus not just in the GI tract,

1365
00:56:52.870 --> 00:56:55.530
but also cervical mucus.

1366
00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:59.100
We can tell different stages of ovulation based on

1367
00:56:59.100 --> 00:57:01.670
what the mucus feels like, and fertility.

1368
00:57:01.670 --> 00:57:04.080
It's really cool stuff.
That's super fascinating.

1369
00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:08.910
Yeah, I mean, I didn't realize the diversity of mucus.

1370
00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:12.690
I always understood it to be a protective, you know,

1371
00:57:12.690 --> 00:57:15.630
system in our body that can neutralize diseases,

1372
00:57:15.630 --> 00:57:17.050
but it's also something

1373
00:57:17.050 --> 00:57:20.220
that we've kind of been conditioned to fear

1374
00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:22.427
because disease spreads through droplets,

1375
00:57:22.427 --> 00:57:25.260
and on the subject of super spreaders,

1376
00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:28.830
you're in an enclosed environment with a lot of people,

1377
00:57:28.830 --> 00:57:30.950
and somebody sneezes or coughs, or, you know,

1378
00:57:30.950 --> 00:57:34.050
mucus is shared communally.

1379
00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:36.723
Like, that's something to be terrified about, right?

1380
00:57:37.580 --> 00:57:42.520
But to the extent that some people's mucus is benign or safe

1381
00:57:42.520 --> 00:57:46.407
versus the person who has the subpar mucus that can,

1382
00:57:46.407 --> 00:57:49.470
in turn, be an accelerant

1383
00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:51.990
to the spread of disease is fascinating.

1384
00:57:51.990 --> 00:57:53.520
So, first of all,

1385
00:57:53.520 --> 00:57:54.720
I mean, the obvious question is,

1386
00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:57.700
like, how do you know if your mucus is doing its job?

1387
00:57:57.700 --> 00:57:59.550
And what are the things that you can do

1388
00:57:59.550 --> 00:58:02.480
to ensure that your, you know, mucus is tip-top?

1389
00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:05.760
I mean, hydration, is it like, you know,

1390
00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:07.430
humidifying your room, diet?
Yeah, and diet.

1391
00:58:07.430 --> 00:58:10.104
Diet plays a huge role, and the humidifying may help

1392
00:58:10.104 --> 00:58:11.820
if you're congested.

1393
00:58:11.820 --> 00:58:12.930
I think the thing about, you know,

1394
00:58:12.930 --> 00:58:14.880
you mentioned about being terrified of mucus,

1395
00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:17.460
other people's mucus isn't as cool as your own mucus.

1396
00:58:17.460 --> 00:58:18.720
It's kind of like other people's stool.

1397
00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:20.480
I don't really want that.
My stool is good, but--

1398
00:58:20.480 --> 00:58:21.980
That's fine, I'm sure it's good.

1399
00:58:21.980 --> 00:58:24.614
You know, I don't need it.

1400
00:58:24.614 --> 00:58:26.250
There are lots of tips.

1401
00:58:26.250 --> 00:58:30.930
You know, one of the things that my team at Avery

1402
00:58:30.930 --> 00:58:34.020
who I've been with them for all the books from,

1403
00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:38.790
they took a chance on me in 2013 with "Gutbliss,"

1404
00:58:38.790 --> 00:58:39.890
all the way through,

1405
00:58:39.890 --> 00:58:42.000
is it really encouraged me

1406
00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:44.190
to provide more actionable information,

1407
00:58:44.190 --> 00:58:45.637
because I'm like, "Oh, this is great.

1408
00:58:45.637 --> 00:58:47.190
"I'm gonna tell people all about this,"

1409
00:58:47.190 --> 00:58:49.320
and, like, that's good to know, but what can I do?

1410
00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:50.153
What am I supposed to do?

1411
00:58:50.153 --> 00:58:51.225
And so, with this,

1412
00:58:51.225 --> 00:58:53.683
it went from being like a little side thing

1413
00:58:53.683 --> 00:58:55.213
with "Gutbliss: Microbiome Solution."

1414
00:58:55.213 --> 00:58:56.670
I had a bigger plan with this one.

