1416
00:58:58.149 --> 00:59:00.949
Page count wise, it might even be closer to a half,

1417
00:59:00.949 --> 00:59:04.200
and to really, you know, think deeply about,

1418
00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:06.120
so, if we think about fever,

1419
00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:07.950
something we haven't gotten into yet,

1420
00:59:07.950 --> 00:59:08.783
what are the guidelines?

1421
00:59:08.783 --> 00:59:11.820
And really pulling those guidelines about

1422
00:59:11.820 --> 00:59:15.270
when to treat a fever, when not, what else can you do?

1423
00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:18.200
Medication section, if you're not taking an NSAID,

1424
00:59:18.200 --> 00:59:19.320
what else could you take?

1425
00:59:19.320 --> 00:59:21.270
And here are some questions to ask your your doctor.

1426
00:59:21.270 --> 00:59:23.310
What about every other day dosing?

1427
00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:25.275
What about these kinds of NSAIDs

1428
00:59:25.275 --> 00:59:28.530
that are not as disruptive to the GI tract?

1429
00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:31.382
So, really trying to feel a little more prescriptive

1430
00:59:31.382 --> 00:59:34.040
for people, as opposed to just, you know, "Don't do this."

1431
00:59:34.040 --> 00:59:35.070
Right.

1432
00:59:35.070 --> 00:59:36.840
Well, let's talk about fever and heat,

1433
00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:38.160
because this is another thing.

1434
00:59:38.160 --> 00:59:39.640
It's like, "Oh, I have an ache."

1435
00:59:39.640 --> 00:59:41.497
"I'll take an Advil."

1436
00:59:41.497 --> 00:59:42.865
"Oh, you know, I'm feeling phlegmy.

1437
00:59:42.865 --> 00:59:44.610
"I'll take a cough suppressant."

1438
00:59:44.610 --> 00:59:48.725
These are all very, you know, knee jerk, common reactions

1439
00:59:48.725 --> 00:59:51.840
to ailments that we've all experienced,

1440
00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:55.297
and on that note, the idea that, when you have a fever,

1441
00:59:55.297 --> 00:59:56.850
"Oh, we need to bring the fever down,"

1442
00:59:56.850 --> 00:59:58.250
like, this is not good, right?

1443
00:59:58.250 --> 01:00:03.250
So, walk us through the protective mechanism of the fever

1444
01:00:03.574 --> 01:00:05.700
and what it's doing to us,

1445
01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:08.024
and when it is appropriate to try to lower it,

1446
01:00:08.024 --> 01:00:10.860
and when we should just let it run its course.

1447
01:00:10.860 --> 01:00:12.570
And I'll tell you, fever's one of those things

1448
01:00:12.570 --> 01:00:15.720
that was good then bad, and may be getting good again.

1449
01:00:15.720 --> 01:00:17.010
So, this idea of, you know,

1450
01:00:17.010 --> 01:00:18.540
you think about different cultures

1451
01:00:18.540 --> 01:00:20.940
and things like sweat lodges, and saunas, et cetera.

1452
01:00:20.940 --> 01:00:23.580
That's all thermal therapy, that's all heat therapy,

1453
01:00:23.580 --> 01:00:25.320
and Hippocrates, I mean,

1454
01:00:25.320 --> 01:00:27.830
everybody's familiar with, all disease begins in the gut,

1455
01:00:27.830 --> 01:00:30.607
but Hippocrates also famously said,

1456
01:00:30.607 --> 01:00:33.600
"Give me a fever and I can cure any illness,"

1457
01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:35.940
and I think it was in 1927,

1458
01:00:35.940 --> 01:00:40.320
an Austrian physician won the Nobel Prize for heat therapy

1459
01:00:40.320 --> 01:00:44.607
that was being used to treat neurosyphilis successfully.

1460
01:00:44.607 --> 01:00:47.100
I mean, of course, we have drugs now

1461
01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:48.720
that are more effective.

1462
01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:50.610
So, heat therapy and this idea of fever

1463
01:00:50.610 --> 01:00:54.450
as being therapeutic is not something new.

1464
01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:57.690
We're seeing now thermal therapy in cancer,

1465
01:00:57.690 --> 01:01:00.652
finding that using heat to treat cancer cells,

1466
01:01:00.652 --> 01:01:03.810
to kill them, is effective.

1467
01:01:03.810 --> 01:01:08.190
Polio virus replicates 250 times faster

1468
01:01:08.190 --> 01:01:11.670
at normal body temperature compared to with a fever.

1469
01:01:11.670 --> 01:01:15.240
So, fever is a really important defense your body has

1470
01:01:15.240 --> 01:01:18.820
of slowing down, if not halting, viral replication,

1471
01:01:18.820 --> 01:01:20.284
and what's the first thing we do

1472
01:01:20.284 --> 01:01:22.610
when we have a virus and a fever?

1473
01:01:22.610 --> 01:01:25.230
Is we reach for an antipyretic,

1474
01:01:25.230 --> 01:01:26.460
something to stop the fever.

1475
01:01:26.460 --> 01:01:29.473
So, understanding that fever is

1476
01:01:29.473 --> 01:01:32.310
a body's sign that there's something going on,

1477
01:01:32.310 --> 01:01:34.562
and also our body's therapeutic response

1478
01:01:34.562 --> 01:01:36.750
to try and handle that thing,

1479
01:01:36.750 --> 01:01:38.010
whatever it is, that's going on.

1480
01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:40.020
So, just the replication rates,

1481
01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:42.600
and so, in the book I do talk about

1482
01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:44.208
when it's okay to take an antipyretic,

1483
01:01:44.208 --> 01:01:46.380
or maybe if you can even wait a little bit,

1484
01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:47.213
and then take it.

1485
01:01:47.213 --> 01:01:48.731
Allow your body time to try

1486
01:01:48.731 --> 01:01:53.731
and get its anti-viral fever virus, you know,

1487
01:01:54.810 --> 01:01:57.990
slowing down replication processes going,

1488
01:01:57.990 --> 01:02:00.360
but there's really interesting data too.

1489
01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:01.200
There's some mice studies.

1490
01:02:01.200 --> 01:02:03.900
If we look particularly at elderly people,

1491
01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:07.560
a lot of the morbidity and mortality around COVID

1492
01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:10.440
with elderly people is this condition ARDS,

1493
01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:12.360
acute respiratory distress syndrome,

1494
01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:14.940
which is essentially lung failure,

1495
01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:19.940
and studies in mice show, that at high heat for a few hours,

1496
01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:25.290
we don't see the respiratory cells dying the same way,

1497
01:02:25.290 --> 01:02:27.120
and then you think about the fact

1498
01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:28.649
that elderly people often are not able

1499
01:02:28.649 --> 01:02:31.350
to mount a high febrile response.

1500
01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.840
Kids can get their temperature up to 105, 106,

1501
01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:35.310
but older people not so much,

1502
01:02:35.310 --> 01:02:37.740
and so, they're looking experimentally now

1503
01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:41.740
at whether thermal therapy might be a treatment for ARDS.

1504
01:02:41.740 --> 01:02:44.370
I mean, this hasn't gone into the human stage yet,

1505
01:02:44.370 --> 01:02:47.125
but they've seen it in mice for slowing down

1506
01:02:47.125 --> 01:02:51.033
the death of the respiratory cells, and then again,

1507
01:02:52.200 --> 01:02:54.150
we've seen similar thermal therapy happening

1508
01:02:54.150 --> 01:02:55.470
in the cancer world.

1509
01:02:55.470 --> 01:02:59.427
So, again, we have to be thoughtful about this

1510
01:02:59.427 --> 01:03:01.800
and we have to understand the process.

1511
01:03:01.800 --> 01:03:03.030
What is our body trying to do?

1512
01:03:03.030 --> 01:03:05.970
And make sure that we're not sabotaging that process

1513
01:03:05.970 --> 01:03:08.040
in our efforts to feel better,

1514
01:03:08.040 --> 01:03:10.050
and there are plenty of things you can do to feel better

1515
01:03:10.050 --> 01:03:12.990
when you have a fever, that can do that

1516
01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:14.185
and make you more comfortable

1517
01:03:14.185 --> 01:03:16.000
without sabotaging that process,

1518
01:03:16.000 --> 01:03:18.870
and also understanding for our kids, you know,

1519
01:03:18.870 --> 01:03:20.070
it's not just a temperature,

1520
01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:21.960
it's, what else is happening with your kid?

1521
01:03:21.960 --> 01:03:24.600
Is your kid listless, not eating, not making eye contact,

1522
01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:28.500
especially with babies, that's a more worrisome sign.

1523
01:03:28.500 --> 01:03:29.991
The kid might have a fever of 104,

1524
01:03:29.991 --> 01:03:33.570
but it's running around, eating, playing, laughing.

1525
01:03:33.570 --> 01:03:34.530
That's a whole different matter.

1526
01:03:34.530 --> 01:03:37.680
So, what else do you look at. and how do you, you know,

1527
01:03:37.680 --> 01:03:38.700
how do you make this evaluation?

1528
01:03:38.700 --> 01:03:40.890
Obviously, this should be done in concert

1529
01:03:40.890 --> 01:03:42.780
with people's healthcare practitioner,

1530
01:03:42.780 --> 01:03:45.570
but giving people some basic information about that

1531
01:03:45.570 --> 01:03:46.597
and some questions to ask.

1532
01:03:46.597 --> 01:03:48.252
Yeah, well, obviously,

1533
01:03:48.252 --> 01:03:50.521
if you have an elevated body temperature,

1534
01:03:50.521 --> 01:03:52.523
and that's slowing replication rates

1535
01:03:52.523 --> 01:03:56.460
of the acute viral infection that you're trying to combat,

1536
01:03:56.460 --> 01:04:00.150
you want to allow that to take place, but I would presume

1537
01:04:00.150 --> 01:04:02.669
that there are other physiological systems

1538
01:04:02.669 --> 01:04:06.030
that get disrupted by an elevated body temperature,

1539
01:04:06.030 --> 01:04:08.970
and at some point when that threshold,

