01:15:27.510 --> 01:15:28.836
Like, we all know people who've

1794
01:15:28.836 --> 01:15:33.836
smoked two packs of Marlboros for 90 years, and are fine,

1795
01:15:34.020 --> 01:15:36.095
but does that mean that cigarettes

1796
01:15:36.095 --> 01:15:38.130
do not increase your risk for lung cancer?

1797
01:15:38.130 --> 01:15:39.180
Of course not.

1798
01:15:39.180 --> 01:15:41.430
So, you know, we have to rely on

1799
01:15:41.430 --> 01:15:43.800
the population based data that we have,

1800
01:15:43.800 --> 01:15:45.630
which is very robust,

1801
01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:50.550
and we know that fiber is a main ingredient

1802
01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:53.190
for creating a healthy microbiome, fiber in fermented food.

1803
01:15:53.190 --> 01:15:56.670
That's really not up for debate.

1804
01:15:56.670 --> 01:15:58.045
Now does it mean that there's not room

1805
01:15:58.045 --> 01:15:59.520
for animal protein on the plate?

1806
01:15:59.520 --> 01:16:00.503
No, it doesn't necessarily mean that.

1807
01:16:00.503 --> 01:16:02.700
There are other reasons to avoid animal protein.

1808
01:16:02.700 --> 01:16:06.510
There environmental reasons, and ethical reasons, et cetera,

1809
01:16:06.510 --> 01:16:09.060
but from a medical lens,

1810
01:16:09.060 --> 01:16:12.000
it's making sure that there's sufficient amounts

1811
01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:14.741
of digestible plant fiber on the plate.

1812
01:16:14.741 --> 01:16:18.300
That is really the most important element of that,

1813
01:16:18.300 --> 01:16:19.260
and then the issue about

1814
01:16:19.260 --> 01:16:20.400
whether you're going to eat meat or not,

1815
01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:23.542
again, that's a more personalized, individualized issue,

1816
01:16:23.542 --> 01:16:26.280
and lots of other factors go into that,

1817
01:16:26.280 --> 01:16:28.920
but there's no debate about the fiber,

1818
01:16:28.920 --> 01:16:30.390
and it doesn't mean that...

1819
01:16:30.390 --> 01:16:32.220
Of course you could be a carnivore your whole life

1820
01:16:32.220 --> 01:16:33.780
and somehow have managed to be fine,

1821
01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:35.782
but you're in n equal one number,

1822
01:16:35.782 --> 01:16:37.933
that does not a scientific study make.

1823
01:16:37.933 --> 01:16:39.502
Right, so, the low hanging fruit

1824
01:16:39.502 --> 01:16:41.500
in terms of things to avoid

1825
01:16:42.910 --> 01:16:45.950
would include artificial sweeteners,

1826
01:16:45.950 --> 01:16:49.567
sugar, fatty foods, alcohol, processed foods, we're all,

1827
01:16:49.567 --> 01:16:52.383
you know, this is all, like, common sense stuff,

1828
01:16:53.220 --> 01:16:56.520
but I loved the example that you gave

1829
01:16:56.520 --> 01:16:58.350
of your patient called Alicia,

1830
01:16:58.350 --> 01:17:00.500
because I feel like that really illustrated

1831
01:17:01.339 --> 01:17:03.702
the interplay between all of these things,

1832
01:17:03.702 --> 01:17:06.005
and how challenging and difficult it can be

1833
01:17:06.005 --> 01:17:10.260
to diagnose somebody who's coming in to you with, you know,

1834
01:17:10.260 --> 01:17:12.500
some sense of something being awry,

1835
01:17:12.500 --> 01:17:14.606
and the process of trying to figure out

1836
01:17:14.606 --> 01:17:17.250
how to untangle that knot.

1837
01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:18.930
Can you tell that story?

1838
01:17:18.930 --> 01:17:19.950
Yes.

1839
01:17:19.950 --> 01:17:24.740
She was the one who was obsessed about fungal organisms,

1840
01:17:24.740 --> 01:17:26.861
and I see this a lot in my practice,

1841
01:17:26.861 --> 01:17:31.170
because, again, there's all this hype

1842
01:17:31.170 --> 01:17:32.003
around different things.

1843
01:17:32.003 --> 01:17:34.200
It's an overgrowth of fungal organisms,

1844
01:17:34.200 --> 01:17:35.610
and you have candida overgrowth,

1845
01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:37.620
and that's what's causing all your problems,

1846
01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:39.360
and it's really balance.

1847
01:17:39.360 --> 01:17:42.330
So, we need yeast in our microbiome.

1848
01:17:42.330 --> 01:17:44.430
Yeast play a really critical role

1849
01:17:44.430 --> 01:17:46.020
in part of the digestive process,

1850
01:17:46.020 --> 01:17:47.520
but we don't want too much yeast,

1851
01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:49.077
and so, when you kill off a lot

1852
01:17:49.077 --> 01:17:51.090
of the healthy bacteria with an antibiotic,

1853
01:17:51.090 --> 01:17:52.230
you leave more room in there,

1854
01:17:52.230 --> 01:17:55.740
and you can get overgrowth of fungal organisms,

1855
01:17:55.740 --> 01:17:58.820
but she had been eating a super restrictive diet,

1856
01:17:58.820 --> 01:18:01.890
essentially no carbohydrates,

1857
01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:06.780
and she was eating a occasional little bit of vegetable,

1858
01:18:06.780 --> 01:18:10.410
green vegetable, no fruit, no grains, tons of meat.

1859
01:18:10.410 --> 01:18:12.207
She'd taken these really potent antifungals,

1860
01:18:12.207 --> 01:18:14.940
and I was really concerned about her liver

1861
01:18:14.940 --> 01:18:17.060
and reminding her that

1862
01:18:17.060 --> 01:18:20.790
the food for our healthy gut bacteria are carbohydrates.

1863
01:18:20.790 --> 01:18:22.226
It's not meat.

1864
01:18:22.226 --> 01:18:25.284
Right, but there's this huge fear of carbohydrates.

1865
01:18:25.284 --> 01:18:27.330
Yeah, and that carbohydrates make you fat,

1866
01:18:27.330 --> 01:18:29.014
and you see the studies coming out

1867
01:18:29.014 --> 01:18:33.103
all the time showing that a primarily plant-based diet,

1868
01:18:33.103 --> 01:18:35.031
whole foods, plant-based diet is

1869
01:18:35.031 --> 01:18:37.470
not just for overall health,

1870
01:18:37.470 --> 01:18:40.650
not just for resiliency to viruses, but also for weight,

1871
01:18:40.650 --> 01:18:42.420
but people somehow have gotten so stuck

1872
01:18:42.420 --> 01:18:46.367
on this idea that carbohydrates are bad.

1873
01:18:46.367 --> 01:18:51.367
Carbohydrates are incredibly important fuel for the gut,

1874
01:18:52.050 --> 01:18:55.530
not just to grow a good gut garden in terms of gut flora,

1875
01:18:55.530 --> 01:18:58.320
but also for providing bulk in the stool,

1876
01:18:58.320 --> 01:18:59.880
so that things can move along,

1877
01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:03.000
and I see these patients coming in on these ketogenic diets,

1878
01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:04.970
incredibly constipated.

1879
01:19:04.970 --> 01:19:07.460
Yeah, that's a common thing.

1880
01:19:07.460 --> 01:19:09.000
Let's talk about sleep.

1881
01:19:09.000 --> 01:19:11.089
Some of the statistics in the sleep section of the book

1882
01:19:11.089 --> 01:19:14.280
are, like, mind blowing.

1883
01:19:14.280 --> 01:19:16.980
Like, what happens in our gut when we're sleep deprived?

1884
01:19:16.980 --> 01:19:21.980
The risk of viral infection skyrockets 88%.

1885
01:19:22.200 --> 01:19:23.550
Is that true?
Crazy.

1886
01:19:23.550 --> 01:19:24.870
I have to say, when I turned in

1887
01:19:24.870 --> 01:19:26.360
the first draft of this book,

1888
01:19:26.360 --> 01:19:29.370
the sleep chapter was 60 pages,

1889
01:19:29.370 --> 01:19:32.014
and the folks at Avery were like,

1890
01:19:32.014 --> 01:19:32.852
"Matthew Walker wrote that book."

1891
01:19:32.852 --> 01:19:34.740
Yeah, you're not Matthew Walker.

1892
01:19:34.740 --> 01:19:37.170
Yeah, "Sean Stevenson wrote that book."

1893
01:19:37.170 --> 01:19:40.410
There are really good sleep books out there,

1894
01:19:40.410 --> 01:19:43.590
but it was so fascinating, Rich,

1895
01:19:43.590 --> 01:19:45.420
and, you know, when you think about it,

1896
01:19:45.420 --> 01:19:49.710
you can go without food a week, and you will be hangry,

1897
01:19:49.710 --> 01:19:51.930
but you can go without food for a week.

1898
01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:54.180
You can go without water for a week,

1899
01:19:54.180 --> 01:19:56.280
and your kidneys will start to shut down,

1900
01:19:56.280 --> 01:19:58.490
but you'll rehydrate, and you'll probably be okay.

1901
01:19:58.490 --> 01:20:01.560
If you go without sleep for a week,

1902
01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:03.603
you will be very close to dead.

1903
01:20:04.770 --> 01:20:06.942
Your testosterone levels will drop,

1904
01:20:06.942 --> 01:20:10.980
your risk for cardiac disease will triple,

1905
01:20:10.980 --> 01:20:11.820
all kinds of things

1906
01:20:11.820 --> 01:20:13.680
will go wrong in your body without sleep.

1907
01:20:13.680 --> 01:20:17.790
So, really, the elixir, sleep is really essential,

1908
01:20:17.790 --> 01:20:20.490
and the sleep-gut connection is

1909
01:20:20.490 --> 01:20:23.850
something that is also really profound.

1910
01:20:23.850 --> 01:20:27.153
Serotonin, which many people know as a feelgood hormone

1911
01:20:27.153 --> 01:20:29.010
is made primarily in our gut,

1912
01:20:29.010 --> 01:20:30.637
about somewhere between 70% and 90%,

1913
01:20:30.637 --> 01:20:33.810
depending on which study you read, of serotonin is produced

1914
01:20:33.810 --> 01:20:36.480
in our gut, primarily by our gut bacteria.

1915
01:20:36.480 --> 01:20:39.307
Well, serotonin is a precursor to melatonin,

1916
01:20:39.307 --> 01:20:40.680
the sleep hormone,

1917
01:20:40.680 --> 01:20:42.807
and so, if your gut bacteria are off,

1918
01:20:42.807 --> 01:20:44.280
and you have a messed up gut,

1919
01:20:44.280 --> 01:20:46.110
and your serotonin production is down,

1920
01:20:46.110 --> 01:20:47.940
which is a real thing we see,

1921
01:20:47.940 --> 01:20:49.161
and that's why there's such a correlation

1922
01:20:49.161 --> 01:20:50.760
between gut disorders,

1923
01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:54.450
and psychiatric disorders and mental health.

1924
01:20:54.450 --> 01:20:55.860
So, if your serotonin is off,

1925
01:20:55.860 --> 01:20:57.168
your melatonin is gonna be off,

1926
01:20:57.168 --> 01:21:01.050
and your sleep is gonna be affected, and vice versa.

1927
01:21:01.050 --> 01:21:03.540
If you're not sleeping well, that's also gonna throw off

1928
01:21:03.540 --> 01:21:05.130
what's going on with your gut bacteria.

1929
01:21:05.130 --> 01:21:07.890
So, one of the things I see, for example,

1930
01:21:07.890 --> 01:21:10.280
is with my college student patients,

1931
01:21:10.280 --> 01:21:14.220
they're always sick around exams when they're not sleeping,

1932
01:21:14.220 --> 01:21:16.445
and for a lot of my patients with Crohn's disease

1933
01:21:16.445 --> 01:21:19.530
and ulcerative colitis, with complex autoimmune diseases,

1934
01:21:19.530 --> 01:21:21.840
they'll tell me that sleep is the thing

1935
01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:24.390
that totally, if the sleep is off,

1936
01:21:24.390 --> 01:21:25.720
they'll have a flare, more than anything else,

1937
01:21:25.720 --> 01:21:27.910
more than their diet, more than stress,

1938
01:21:27.910 --> 01:21:30.810
more than anything else, is really the sleep.

1939
01:21:30.810 --> 01:21:34.230
Less than four hours of sleep correlates

1940
01:21:34.230 --> 01:21:38.280
to a 7% drop in critical,

1941
01:21:38.280 --> 01:21:40.620
no, I think it was 70% drop

1942
01:21:40.620 --> 01:21:42.959
in critical immune cells the next day.

1943
01:21:42.959 --> 01:21:43.871
That's absolutely right.
So, this happens

1944
01:21:43.871 --> 01:21:44.705
very quickly.

1945
01:21:44.705 --> 01:21:46.256
It's the reason why

1946
01:21:46.256 --> 01:21:48.625
I'm not taking the red eye back tomorrow.

1947
01:21:48.625 --> 01:21:52.560
I'm done tomorrow afternoon, too late to catch the 4:45

1948
01:21:52.560 --> 01:21:56.460
to get back to DC, but in time for the red eye,

1949
01:21:56.460 --> 01:21:57.877
but I know I won't sleep, and I'm like,

1950
01:21:57.877 --> 01:21:59.947
"No, I'll take the 7:55 in the morning.

1951
01:21:59.947 --> 01:22:02.070
"I am not risking that."

1952
01:22:02.070 --> 01:22:06.290
There's crazy data about sleep and vaccines too.

1953
01:22:06.290 --> 01:22:10.410
So, if you are sleep deprived before your vaccine,

1954
01:22:10.410 --> 01:22:14.160
the effectiveness is significantly reduced,

1955
01:22:14.160 --> 01:22:16.050
and our daughter who's a rower,

1956
01:22:16.050 --> 01:22:17.730
when she was getting her first vaccine,

1957
01:22:17.730 --> 01:22:20.070
it was during the regatta season

1958
01:22:20.070 --> 01:22:22.650
and she had to get up at like 4:00 A.M., or something,

1959
01:22:22.650 --> 01:22:24.420
I forget where we were in Philly,

1960
01:22:24.420 --> 01:22:26.303
or I was going somewhere the next day,

1961
01:22:26.303 --> 01:22:28.537
and I'm like, "Nope, we've got to reschedule that.

1962
01:22:28.537 --> 01:22:30.067
"There's no way you're getting a vaccine

1963
01:22:30.067 --> 01:22:32.337
"after, you know, four and a half hours sleep,"

1964
01:22:32.337 --> 01:22:35.700
and so, there's really clear data, not just in older people,

1965
01:22:35.700 --> 01:22:38.850
but in everyone, that being sleep deprived

1966
01:22:38.850 --> 01:22:42.930
in the 48 hours before you get a vaccine

1967
01:22:42.930 --> 01:22:44.760
can lead to a decrease in effectiveness,

1968
01:22:44.760 --> 01:22:46.240
in some studies by as much as 30%,

1969
01:22:46.240 --> 01:22:47.600
and that's not just for the COVID vaccine,

1970
01:22:47.600 --> 01:22:50.181
that's also for hepatitis vaccines,

1971
01:22:50.181 --> 01:22:53.190
for flu shots, et cetera, and, you know,

1972
01:22:53.190 --> 01:22:54.720
it's just not something that I think

1973
01:22:54.720 --> 01:22:58.620
is even in our awareness, this idea that it would matter.

1974
01:22:58.620 --> 01:23:01.020
We just think, you get the vaccine, it works,

1975
01:23:01.020 --> 01:23:02.443
but this is one of these factors,

1976
01:23:02.443 --> 01:23:04.860
and when you look at some of the statistics

1977
01:23:04.860 --> 01:23:07.920
in the book that you just read, the drop in immune function

1978
01:23:07.920 --> 01:23:09.870
when you're sleep deprived,

1979
01:23:09.870 --> 01:23:12.471
you really start to see how it's all connected.

1980
01:23:12.471 --> 01:23:13.950
Yeah, it's unbelievable.

1981
01:23:13.950 --> 01:23:15.147
The other one that hit me really hard

1982
01:23:15.147 --> 01:23:20.147
was every hour that you sleep is associated with 12%,

1983
01:23:22.273 --> 01:23:24.600
a 12% reduction in the odds

1984
01:23:24.600 --> 01:23:28.213
of becoming infected by viral infection.

1985
01:23:28.213 --> 01:23:30.960
I mean, infected means, how are you defining that?

1986
01:23:30.960 --> 01:23:33.450
Because exposure--
Meaning ill, becoming ill.

1987
01:23:33.450 --> 01:23:35.340
Becoming ill, okay.
Yeah, becoming ill,

1988
01:23:35.340 --> 01:23:38.310
but, you know, I think we can all think about

1989
01:23:38.310 --> 01:23:40.530
times in our life when we've been, you know,

1990
01:23:40.530 --> 01:23:43.500
for me it was every year before a big GI conference,

1991
01:23:43.500 --> 01:23:46.290
Digestive Disease Week, which happens in May,

1992
01:23:46.290 --> 01:23:48.699
and typically, especially when I was still full-time

1993
01:23:48.699 --> 01:23:50.400
at the hospital at Georgetown,

1994
01:23:50.400 --> 01:23:51.240
and so, I'd be working on

1995
01:23:51.240 --> 01:23:53.010
five or six different presentations,

1996
01:23:53.010 --> 01:23:54.750
and, you know, it's like, you know this stuff is

1997
01:23:54.750 --> 01:23:55.860
coming six months in advance,

1998
01:23:55.860 --> 01:23:56.910
but it's still last minute.

1999
01:23:56.910 --> 01:23:57.743
You're getting slides together,

2000
01:23:57.743 --> 01:24:00.850
and you're, you know, finishing stuff up, and not sleeping,

2001
01:24:00.850 --> 01:24:03.750
and invariably, I'd get sick, and you know, as I said,

2002
01:24:03.750 --> 01:24:05.100
I see it with my college students

2003
01:24:05.100 --> 01:24:07.290
who have chronic autoimmune diseases.

2004
01:24:07.290 --> 01:24:08.123
You see it.

2005
01:24:08.123 --> 01:24:10.620
You're run down, and you're more likely to get sick,

2006
01:24:10.620 --> 01:24:14.310
and we know that in the back of our minds intellectually,

2007
01:24:14.310 --> 01:24:16.410
but when you're run down, you're tired,

2008
01:24:16.410 --> 01:24:18.330
you're not sleeping well, you're more likely to get sick,

2009
01:24:18.330 --> 01:24:23.330
but I think we're not aware how profound that impact is,

2010
01:24:24.155 --> 01:24:28.020
and I think, Matthew Walker, when he was on,

2011
01:24:28.020 --> 01:24:30.770
talked about this incredible global study

2012
01:24:30.770 --> 01:24:32.610
with daylight savings time,

2013
01:24:32.610 --> 01:24:34.500
and how we see this tremendous increase,

2014
01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:35.724
I think, up to a 25%,

2015
01:24:35.724 --> 01:24:38.700
24%, 25% increase in heart attacks

2016
01:24:38.700 --> 01:24:40.740
the day after we all lose an hour of sleep,

2017
01:24:40.740 --> 01:24:44.303
and then a drop, not as dramatic, but significant.

2018
01:24:44.303 --> 01:24:46.393
And then when you think about residents,

2019
01:24:46.393 --> 01:24:49.740
med school residents working these insane shifts

2020
01:24:49.740 --> 01:24:51.900
in hospitals where they become

2021
01:24:51.900 --> 01:24:55.200
more susceptible to disease and infection,

2022
01:24:55.200 --> 01:24:57.043
then, of course, they're gonna be more likely

2023
01:24:57.043 --> 01:25:00.900
to shed and spread it when they're doing their rounds.

2024
01:25:00.900 --> 01:25:04.440
Go into any hospital physician's lounge,

2025
01:25:04.440 --> 01:25:05.790
and look at what's in there.

2026
01:25:05.790 --> 01:25:08.940
It's soda, and donuts, and ham sandwiches.

2027
01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:10.173
Why can't we solve that problem?

2028
01:25:10.173 --> 01:25:12.060
And, you know, Neil Bernard

2029
01:25:12.060 --> 01:25:12.893
and his wonderful group,

2030
01:25:12.893 --> 01:25:15.180
Physicians Committee for Responsible Medicine

2031
01:25:15.180 --> 01:25:17.460
are doing a petition now.

2032
01:25:17.460 --> 01:25:19.920
They've been helping them with the folks at Georgetown

2033
01:25:19.920 --> 01:25:24.920
to remove known carcinogens from the hospital meal tray.

2034
01:25:25.260 --> 01:25:28.050
So, sausage, bacon, I mean,

2035
01:25:28.050 --> 01:25:29.040
you could argue about whether

2036
01:25:29.040 --> 01:25:31.620
you are gonna serve animal protein or not in hospitals,

2037
01:25:31.620 --> 01:25:34.080
but you cannot make an argument for why you would be

2038
01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:38.550
serving sausage and bacon to sick people in the hospital.

2039
01:25:38.550 --> 01:25:42.710
My dad had a cardiac catheterization about five years ago,

2040
01:25:42.710 --> 01:25:45.990
had an acute event playing tennis, and went in,

2041
01:25:45.990 --> 01:25:49.320
and had state-of-the-art care at Washington Hospital Center,

2042
01:25:49.320 --> 01:25:50.880
incredible cardiac services,

2043
01:25:50.880 --> 01:25:55.410
and they went right in, aborted the heart attack, fantastic.

2044
01:25:55.410 --> 01:25:57.223
I was in New Haven at a college reunion.

2045
01:25:57.223 --> 01:26:00.920
I rushed back, he's sitting there, he's lying in the bed,

2046
01:26:00.920 --> 01:26:04.560
there's a hamburger on his plate.

2047
01:26:04.560 --> 01:26:06.348
This is what they're serving him

2048
01:26:06.348 --> 01:26:09.570
after an acute cardiac event, you know, out of

2049
01:26:09.570 --> 01:26:11.670
the cath lab, recovered.
That's total insanity.

2050
01:26:11.670 --> 01:26:12.660
Insanity.

2051
01:26:12.660 --> 01:26:15.200
Why is it so difficult to solve this problem?

2052
01:26:15.200 --> 01:26:19.945
Like, where's the missing link in resolving this equation?

2053
01:26:19.945 --> 01:26:21.500
I think it's--
Is it the money?

2054
01:26:21.500 --> 01:26:22.880
Is it government contracts?

2055
01:26:22.880 --> 01:26:27.880
It's disassociation between acute treatment of illness

2056
01:26:31.230 --> 01:26:35.110
and prevention of illness, and what we have seen, Rich,

2057
01:26:35.110 --> 01:26:36.450
is that the medical community

2058
01:26:36.450 --> 01:26:38.490
has not done a good job on the prevention side.

2059
01:26:38.490 --> 01:26:40.120
I think we can all agree.

2060
01:26:40.120 --> 01:26:42.570
We're really good with the acute side.

2061
01:26:42.570 --> 01:26:45.010
I am thrilled that Washington Hospital Center was available,

2062
01:26:45.010 --> 01:26:46.110
and 10 minutes away,

2063
01:26:46.110 --> 01:26:47.940
and they did a fantastic job with my dad,

2064
01:26:47.940 --> 01:26:50.040
and I do credit them for that,

2065
01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:52.275
them and his cardiologist Jack Flyer,

2066
01:26:52.275 --> 01:26:54.021
for why he's alive at 87,

2067
01:26:54.021 --> 01:26:57.240
and doing well, and eating lots of lentils,

2068
01:26:57.240 --> 01:27:00.687
but the prevention side, we don't do,

2069
01:27:00.687 --> 01:27:02.253
and when you think about it,

2070
01:27:03.470 --> 01:27:08.100
having people be ill keeps these organizations in business.

2071
01:27:08.100 --> 01:27:10.200
Now, I don't think that they're thinking about it that way.

2072
01:27:10.200 --> 01:27:11.647
They're not like, "Oh, we hope he goes out

2073
01:27:11.647 --> 01:27:12.720
"and has another cardiac event."

2074
01:27:12.720 --> 01:27:13.553
Not at all.

2075
01:27:13.553 --> 01:27:15.810
It's just that it's not on their radar.

2076
01:27:15.810 --> 01:27:20.370
They're in the business of the acute care and interventions,

2077
01:27:20.370 --> 01:27:22.200
and they're not in the business of prevention,

2078
01:27:22.200 --> 01:27:27.200
and we've seen these other industries that have flourished,

2079
01:27:27.360 --> 01:27:29.220
health coaches and other people

2080
01:27:29.220 --> 01:27:30.660
who are really focused on that,

2081
01:27:30.660 --> 01:27:34.110
on the diet and lifestyle, and I think it's wonderful,

2082
01:27:34.110 --> 01:27:36.150
and it has arisen out of a great need,

2083
01:27:36.150 --> 01:27:37.830
which is a fact that we've neglected it,

2084
01:27:37.830 --> 01:27:41.080
and the medical visit is fast, hospital-based medicine,

2085
01:27:41.080 --> 01:27:42.730
so you don't have a long time.

2086
01:27:42.730 --> 01:27:47.690
So, we needed to have other resources for people,

2087
01:27:47.690 --> 01:27:51.210
other places for people to go to to learn about that.

2088
01:27:51.210 --> 01:27:54.275
Yeah, you kind of raise the conspiracy flag

2089
01:27:54.275 --> 01:27:56.302
of, you know, the system is set up

2090
01:27:56.302 --> 01:27:59.250
to keep sick people sick,

2091
01:27:59.250 --> 01:28:01.140
because that's where the money is getting made.

2092
01:28:01.140 --> 01:28:04.500
I think what gets confused in that conversation is

2093
01:28:04.500 --> 01:28:07.350
this idea of mustache twirling, you know,

2094
01:28:07.350 --> 01:28:09.840
executives sitting around a table like this,

2095
01:28:09.840 --> 01:28:12.510
conspiring to, you know, extract money

2096
01:28:12.510 --> 01:28:15.425
from the woe-begotten consumer.

2097
01:28:15.425 --> 01:28:16.470
It's not that.

2098
01:28:16.470 --> 01:28:17.600
It's just that there is

2099
01:28:17.600 --> 01:28:21.150
a certain system in place that's very profitable,

2100
01:28:21.150 --> 01:28:23.610
and when that is the case,

2101
01:28:23.610 --> 01:28:25.950
there are many parties that are invested

2102
01:28:25.950 --> 01:28:28.127
in maintaining the integrity of that system,

2103
01:28:28.127 --> 01:28:31.500
and it becomes more and more difficult to make changes,

2104
01:28:31.500 --> 01:28:34.990
but to your point, the explosion

2105
01:28:34.990 --> 01:28:39.009
of functional integrative medicine practitioners is

2106
01:28:39.009 --> 01:28:41.700
very heartwarming, and they are serving this need,

2107
01:28:41.700 --> 01:28:44.910
and I think it's cool to see that blossom.

2108
01:28:44.910 --> 01:28:49.050
They found a way to make it economically viable.

2109
01:28:49.050 --> 01:28:51.090
I think that was the big impediment, right?

2110
01:28:51.090 --> 01:28:53.850
Like, how are you gonna be able to make a living doing this

2111
01:28:53.850 --> 01:28:55.840
when the system is set up

2112
01:28:56.700 --> 01:28:59.580
such that you need to see x many patients a day

2113
01:28:59.580 --> 01:29:02.756
for 15 minutes each, because of the way insurance companies

2114
01:29:02.756 --> 01:29:06.060
have, you know, created the reimbursement schedule.

2115
01:29:06.060 --> 01:29:07.820
And it's not just a, you know,

2116
01:29:07.820 --> 01:29:11.130
at the practitioner level, in terms of physician level,

2117
01:29:11.130 --> 01:29:13.020
but it's really people like health coaches

2118
01:29:13.020 --> 01:29:15.300
who are much more grassroots,

2119
01:29:15.300 --> 01:29:18.556
who can do the hand holding, and the explaining,

2120
01:29:18.556 --> 01:29:20.550
and the walking through and, you know,

2121
01:29:20.550 --> 01:29:23.400
really have that frequent interaction

2122
01:29:23.400 --> 01:29:25.980
because, you know, gastroenterologists are really good

2123
01:29:25.980 --> 01:29:28.230
at doing colonoscopies and endoscopes,

2124
01:29:28.230 --> 01:29:32.430
but most gastroenterology programs even today

2125
01:29:32.430 --> 01:29:35.070
don't have formal training in nutrition,

2126
01:29:35.070 --> 01:29:38.190
really significant training in terms of, you know,

2127
01:29:38.190 --> 01:29:39.387
explaining to somebody, what's the difference

2128
01:29:39.387 --> 01:29:40.618
between gluten intolerance,

2129
01:29:40.618 --> 01:29:43.660
and a wheat allergy, and celiac disease?

2130
01:29:43.660 --> 01:29:45.180
And even when we know it,

2131
01:29:45.180 --> 01:29:47.610
and I think most gastroenterologists do know that,

2132
01:29:47.610 --> 01:29:50.220
but do we have 45 minutes to sit down

2133
01:29:50.220 --> 01:29:51.960
and explain the difference to somebody

2134
01:29:51.960 --> 01:29:54.340
who's just been diagnosed with celiac disease

2135
01:29:55.235 --> 01:29:57.870
versus somebody who is just gluten intolerant,

2136
01:29:57.870 --> 01:29:59.820
I shouldn't say, just, but somebody who's gluten intolerant,

2137
01:29:59.820 --> 01:30:01.560
that these are very separate conditions

2138
01:30:01.560 --> 01:30:04.110
and they require very different approaches,

2139
01:30:04.110 --> 01:30:06.780
and so, you know, I think of it as these, you know,

2140
01:30:06.780 --> 01:30:09.870
grassroots community based folks

2141
01:30:09.870 --> 01:30:12.270
who can really do that heavy lifting.

2142
01:30:12.270 --> 01:30:15.120
I would like to see more disease specific training

2143
01:30:15.120 --> 01:30:16.470
for some of the health coaches.

2144
01:30:16.470 --> 01:30:17.940
A lot of 'em have great empathy,

2145
01:30:17.940 --> 01:30:20.400
but maybe not as much specific training

2146
01:30:20.400 --> 01:30:23.940
in the different areas, and I think when we get that...

2147
01:30:23.940 --> 01:30:25.740
So, we have like a whole army of, you know,

2148
01:30:25.740 --> 01:30:29.940
diabetes educators, or dysbiosis educators,

2149
01:30:29.940 --> 01:30:32.970
people who help people understand gallbladder disease

2150
01:30:32.970 --> 01:30:35.190
and what to do so you can hold onto your gallbladder

2151
01:30:35.190 --> 01:30:37.320
if you have gallstones, and not have to have it removed.

2152
01:30:37.320 --> 01:30:42.210
So, we start to see more of that disease specific training

2153
01:30:42.210 --> 01:30:43.043
with health coaches.

2154
01:30:43.043 --> 01:30:45.390
I think we'll really start to move the needle.

2155
01:30:45.390 --> 01:30:48.240
Yeah, and the follow up and the accountability

2156
01:30:48.240 --> 01:30:49.906
that's so critical to actually

2157
01:30:49.906 --> 01:30:52.170
getting somebody on a better path.

2158
01:30:52.170 --> 01:30:54.330
And the working with the medical community,

2159
01:30:54.330 --> 01:30:56.580
so that it's not either, or,

2160
01:30:56.580 --> 01:30:58.740
but you are working with a health coach,

2161
01:30:58.740 --> 01:31:00.220
health coaches attached to medical practices.

2162
01:31:00.220 --> 01:31:03.360
Like, the gastroenterologist might diagnose somebody

2163
01:31:03.360 --> 01:31:06.000
with celiac disease or gallstones,

2164
01:31:06.000 --> 01:31:07.290
and then they refer them to the health coach,

2165
01:31:07.290 --> 01:31:08.940
and then the health coach does all that work

2166
01:31:08.940 --> 01:31:10.197
with the diet and lifestyle, the sleep,

2167
01:31:10.197 --> 01:31:12.000
the stress, the food,

2168
01:31:12.000 --> 01:31:15.010
and then there's more cross pollination between the two.

2169
01:31:15.010 --> 01:31:17.040
So, that's something that I would love to see,

2170
01:31:17.040 --> 01:31:18.690
and I think we're moving towards it

2171
01:31:18.690 --> 01:31:21.370
because I think consumers are demanding it.

2172
01:31:21.370 --> 01:31:25.920
Let's talk about weight, specifically, obesity.

2173
01:31:25.920 --> 01:31:30.510
I think we're all aware that obesity was

2174
01:31:30.510 --> 01:31:32.578
the biggest comorbidity factor

2175
01:31:32.578 --> 01:31:35.550
that was driving severe COVID cases.

2176
01:31:35.550 --> 01:31:37.830
Some of the statistics in your book, you know,

2177
01:31:37.830 --> 01:31:42.140
are pretty heavy.

2178
01:31:42.140 --> 01:31:46.470
78% of patients hospitalized for COVID were obese.

2179
01:31:46.470 --> 01:31:51.470
113%, were 113% more likely

2180
01:31:51.960 --> 01:31:55.260
to be hospitalized for COVID if you were obese,

2181
01:31:55.260 --> 01:31:56.337
but the interesting thing to me

2182
01:31:56.337 --> 01:31:58.515
that I'd never really considered is

2183
01:31:58.515 --> 01:32:03.515
the extent to which obesity becomes a public health crisis.

2184
01:32:05.820 --> 01:32:08.983
So, explain that, because I've never really pondered that.

2185
01:32:08.983 --> 01:32:10.800
I tell you that was pretty shocking to me too.

2186
01:32:10.800 --> 01:32:12.420
So, if you think about obesity,

2187
01:32:12.420 --> 01:32:15.090
what the risk is to the individual,

2188
01:32:15.090 --> 01:32:17.040
we know with something like COVID,

2189
01:32:17.040 --> 01:32:19.140
so you're gonna have trouble ventilating the lungs,

2190
01:32:19.140 --> 01:32:21.840
expanding the lungs, because of the adiposity,

2191
01:32:21.840 --> 01:32:24.780
because of the pressure on the chest if you're obese,

2192
01:32:24.780 --> 01:32:27.480
so you have issues with ventilation,

2193
01:32:27.480 --> 01:32:30.000
you're going to have issues with potential complications

2194
01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:33.964
like clotting, because increased weight

2195
01:32:33.964 --> 01:32:35.430
in the setting of obesity is

2196
01:32:35.430 --> 01:32:37.770
associated with increased clotting factors

2197
01:32:37.770 --> 01:32:38.829
and other inflammatory things floating

2198
01:32:38.829 --> 01:32:40.370
around in the bloodstream,

2199
01:32:40.370 --> 01:32:41.880
and you're going to have

2200
01:32:41.880 --> 01:32:44.340
an altered immune response, because adipose tissue,

2201
01:32:44.340 --> 01:32:47.730
fatty tissue is itself immunologically active

2202
01:32:47.730 --> 01:32:49.140
in a negative way,

2203
01:32:49.140 --> 01:32:51.300
and can lead to an overblown immune response.

2204
01:32:51.300 --> 01:32:54.570
Those are all things that can affect the individual,

2205
01:32:54.570 --> 01:32:57.700
but in terms of what it means for society,

2206
01:32:57.700 --> 01:32:59.871
having obesity is also associated

2207
01:32:59.871 --> 01:33:02.009
with prolonged viral shedding.

2208
01:33:02.009 --> 01:33:03.810
We've seen that for influenza,

2209
01:33:03.810 --> 01:33:06.750
we've seen it for SARS-CoV-2, and many other viruses.

2210
01:33:06.750 --> 01:33:08.980
You shed the virus longer.

2211
01:33:08.980 --> 01:33:11.223
What that means if you shed the virus longer is,

2212
01:33:11.223 --> 01:33:13.500
that there's more opportunity potentially

2213
01:33:13.500 --> 01:33:14.450
to infect somebody,

2214
01:33:14.450 --> 01:33:16.195
and there's also more opportunity

2215
01:33:16.195 --> 01:33:19.560
for the virus to become more virulent during that process,

2216
01:33:19.560 --> 01:33:22.140
and so, from a public health point of view,

2217
01:33:22.140 --> 01:33:23.031
if you have a population

2218
01:33:23.031 --> 01:33:27.870
with a large percentage of people in it who have obesity,

2219
01:33:27.870 --> 01:33:30.240
you are potentially going to end up

2220
01:33:30.240 --> 01:33:34.780
with a more virulent viral outbreak over time.

2221
01:33:34.780 --> 01:33:38.465
Yeah, the shedding with influenza

2222
01:33:38.465 --> 01:33:42.021
was 42% longer for people who were obese.

2223
01:33:42.021 --> 01:33:43.950
So, it's not a small margin.

2224
01:33:43.950 --> 01:33:45.010
Yeah, that's right,

2225
01:33:45.010 --> 01:33:47.400
and that was stunning to me also,

2226
01:33:47.400 --> 01:33:50.440
and it also makes me think about, you know, we saw a lot

2227
01:33:51.690 --> 01:33:54.507
of collective concern, and people coming together

2228
01:33:54.507 --> 01:33:58.280
in goodwill during this pandemic, particularly early on,

2229
01:33:58.280 --> 01:34:02.460
and so, realizing that this is a problem that we all share,

2230
01:34:02.460 --> 01:34:05.880
that we all have a responsibility

2231
01:34:05.880 --> 01:34:07.440
to figure out how we can help,

2232
01:34:07.440 --> 01:34:10.680
whether it's individually or from a policy level,

2233
01:34:10.680 --> 01:34:13.830
make sure that people who struggle with obesity

2234
01:34:13.830 --> 01:34:16.390
have access to better food,

2235
01:34:16.390 --> 01:34:19.170
safe places to exercise, stress reduction,

2236
01:34:19.170 --> 01:34:21.660
all the different things that contribute to it,

2237
01:34:21.660 --> 01:34:24.810
because, A, it's the right thing to do,

2238
01:34:24.810 --> 01:34:29.810
and B, because it has a ripple effect on the whole society.

2239
01:34:30.060 --> 01:34:32.610
Of all the comorbidities,

2240
01:34:32.610 --> 01:34:35.940
why is obesity such a powerful one

2241
01:34:35.940 --> 01:34:38.980
when it comes to susceptibility to viral infection?

2242
01:34:38.980 --> 01:34:40.834
I think, Richard, it's because of

2243
01:34:40.834 --> 01:34:43.170
all the different elements it effects.

2244
01:34:43.170 --> 01:34:47.850
So, there's a mechanical, anatomical issue with the lungs,

2245
01:34:47.850 --> 01:34:49.860
and when you can't expand your lungs,

2246
01:34:49.860 --> 01:34:52.760
you end up having collapsed alveoli in the lungs,

2247
01:34:52.760 --> 01:34:54.796
and the bacteria there start to produce,

2248
01:34:54.796 --> 01:34:57.300
and you end up with secondary infection,

2249
01:34:57.300 --> 01:34:58.890
bacterial super infection.

2250
01:34:58.890 --> 01:35:01.860
So, having a bacterial pneumonia on top of COVID

2251
01:35:01.860 --> 01:35:03.330
can be really deadly.

2252
01:35:03.330 --> 01:35:05.760
So, with obesity, there are mechanical factors,

2253
01:35:05.760 --> 01:35:09.520
there are physiological factors, there are hormonal factors,

2254
01:35:09.520 --> 01:35:12.930
there are endocrine factors, there are immune factors,

2255
01:35:12.930 --> 01:35:16.380
so it's a condition that affects, you know,

2256
01:35:16.380 --> 01:35:19.080
every aspect of our body's physiology,

2257
01:35:19.080 --> 01:35:20.760
and I think that, unlike heart disease,

2258
01:35:20.760 --> 01:35:22.745
where, okay, heart disease primarily affects your heart,

2259
01:35:22.745 --> 01:35:24.603
it may affect your circulation,

2260
01:35:26.310 --> 01:35:28.680
diabetes is primarily an endocrine disease,

2261
01:35:28.680 --> 01:35:31.290
but with obesity and, of course,

2262
01:35:31.290 --> 01:35:32.670
our risk for all of these things,

2263
01:35:32.670 --> 01:35:34.950
heart disease, cardiometabolic disease,

2264
01:35:34.950 --> 01:35:36.900
cancer, et cetera, are increased.

2265
01:35:36.900 --> 01:35:39.993
I mean, obesity doesn't live in a vacuum.

2266
01:35:39.993 --> 01:35:41.046
It's usually associated

2267
01:35:41.046 --> 01:35:44.430
with some other battery of, you know...

2268
01:35:44.430 --> 01:35:46.410
Absolutely.
Poor health outcomes.

2269
01:35:46.410 --> 01:35:48.871
And it's also very strongly correlated

2270
01:35:48.871 --> 01:35:50.760
with changes in the microbiome.

2271
01:35:50.760 --> 01:35:55.020
We can look at microbial sample

2272
01:35:55.020 --> 01:35:57.090
and we can predict with pretty good accuracy

2273
01:35:57.090 --> 01:35:58.890
whether this person has obesity or not

2274
01:35:58.890 --> 01:36:01.860
based on the complemented bacteria.

2275
01:36:01.860 --> 01:36:03.870
So, it's strongly associated,

2276
01:36:03.870 --> 01:36:06.030
but I can't say that's the factor.

2277
01:36:06.030 --> 01:36:07.221
It's the fact that it is, you know,

2278
01:36:07.221 --> 01:36:09.060
effecting so many different--
And you mentioned something

2279
01:36:09.060 --> 01:36:11.820
about adipose tissue specifically.

2280
01:36:11.820 --> 01:36:12.653
Sorry, I interrupted you,

2281
01:36:12.653 --> 01:36:14.520
I didn't mean to.
No, no worries.

2282
01:36:14.520 --> 01:36:17.013
Something about the adipose tissue specifically

2283
01:36:17.013 --> 01:36:20.640
that that has a negative impact on immune response.

2284
01:36:20.640 --> 01:36:23.790
Yes, the adipose tissue can itself

2285
01:36:23.790 --> 01:36:26.340
release more immune cells more than you need.

2286
01:36:26.340 --> 01:36:29.610
This whole concept of overshooting and cytokine storm.

2287
01:36:29.610 --> 01:36:32.790
So, that's another potential complication there

2288
01:36:32.790 --> 01:36:34.690
for people who have obesity.

2289
01:36:34.690 --> 01:36:35.753
Well, let's talk about

2290
01:36:35.753 --> 01:36:39.420
getting outdoors, exposure to nature.

2291
01:36:39.420 --> 01:36:41.370
Bathing in the biome, right?

2292
01:36:41.370 --> 01:36:45.890
This is a big part of taking care of our gut health,

2293
01:36:45.890 --> 01:36:49.203
is our exposure to our natural environments,

2294
01:36:50.460 --> 01:36:53.220
and another statistic that jumped out to me

2295
01:36:53.220 --> 01:36:56.610
from the book on this was what they discovered

2296
01:36:56.610 --> 01:37:00.030
in the wake of the 1918 Spanish flu epidemic.

2297
01:37:00.030 --> 01:37:01.230
That was fascinating.

2298
01:37:01.230 --> 01:37:02.880
So, you know, we look back 100 years,

2299
01:37:02.880 --> 01:37:05.040
more than 100 years now,

2300
01:37:05.040 --> 01:37:08.970
and we found that this concept of the open air factor,

2301
01:37:08.970 --> 01:37:12.180
and it's defined as a germicidal constituent in open air,

2302
01:37:12.180 --> 01:37:14.700
that it's basically, factors in open air,

2303
01:37:14.700 --> 01:37:16.800
many of which we're not sure what they are,

2304
01:37:16.800 --> 01:37:20.630
that are lethal to viruses and certain pathogenic bacteria,

2305
01:37:20.630 --> 01:37:22.710
and in the the Spanish flu epidemic,

2306
01:37:22.710 --> 01:37:25.920
what they found is that people who recovered outside

2307
01:37:25.920 --> 01:37:27.470
had a much lower mortality,

2308
01:37:27.470 --> 01:37:30.900
and when we look at it in the military setting,

2309
01:37:30.900 --> 01:37:33.282
officers were often given a hospital bed

2310
01:37:33.282 --> 01:37:34.530
because they were officers,

2311
01:37:34.530 --> 01:37:38.283
and the enlisted men were put in cots outside the hospital

2312
01:37:38.283 --> 01:37:40.000
when the hospitals were overflowing.

2313
01:37:40.000 --> 01:37:41.910
Well, the mortality was very different.

2314
01:37:41.910 --> 01:37:43.080
In some of those studies,

2315
01:37:43.080 --> 01:37:47.470
the officers recuperating inside had up to a 40% mortality

2316
01:37:47.470 --> 01:37:50.061
and the enlisted soldiers on the cots outside

2317
01:37:50.061 --> 01:37:52.040
had a 13% mortality.

2318
01:37:52.040 --> 01:37:53.450
That's a massive difference.

2319
01:37:53.450 --> 01:37:56.000
That's a massive difference, and so, we see that,

2320
01:37:56.000 --> 01:37:59.500
if you look at the impact of something like forest bathing,

2321
01:37:59.500 --> 01:38:02.390
and the exact Japanese term escapes me at the moment.

2322
01:38:02.390 --> 01:38:05.610
It's something shinrin, oh, I'll mess it up,

2323
01:38:05.610 --> 01:38:09.540
but we know from a couple different studies that

2324
01:38:09.540 --> 01:38:11.033
that lowers blood pressure,

2325
01:38:11.033 --> 01:38:14.130
it decreases risk for heart disease,

2326
01:38:14.130 --> 01:38:16.230
it increases feelings of wellbeing,

2327
01:38:16.230 --> 01:38:18.960
it decreases feelings of stress, et cetera,

2328
01:38:18.960 --> 01:38:21.720
but it turns out from an immune point of view,

2329
01:38:21.720 --> 01:38:23.470
it also does some pretty amazing things too.

2330
01:38:23.470 --> 01:38:25.409
So, this open air factor is different

2331
01:38:25.409 --> 01:38:27.600
from just sunlight and Vitamin D.

2332
01:38:27.600 --> 01:38:29.340
Sunlight and Vitamin D is a thing for sure,

2333
01:38:29.340 --> 01:38:30.600
but it's open air,

2334
01:38:30.600 --> 01:38:33.690
and so, if you can't get outside and stroll in the woods,

2335
01:38:33.690 --> 01:38:35.384
you can open your car window.

2336
01:38:35.384 --> 01:38:37.800
I'm going like this, but it's more like this.

2337
01:38:37.800 --> 01:38:39.030
Pressing the button, rolling it down.

2338
01:38:39.030 --> 01:38:41.070
Nobody's car window opens out anymore.

2339
01:38:41.070 --> 01:38:43.410
You can open a car window, you can open a door,

2340
01:38:43.410 --> 01:38:46.050
you can literally sit on a window ledge,

2341
01:38:46.050 --> 01:38:49.410
and it's been striking even in my own life

2342
01:38:49.410 --> 01:38:52.560
how much time I spent inside during the pandemic.

2343
01:38:52.560 --> 01:38:53.850
We're really lucky to live

2344
01:38:53.850 --> 01:38:55.290
right on the edge of Rock Creek Park.

2345
01:38:55.290 --> 01:38:56.621
We moved since you were there last.

2346
01:38:56.621 --> 01:39:00.270
Literally, the trailhead is right outside our door,

2347
01:39:00.270 --> 01:39:02.100
so it's really easy for us to get outside,

2348
01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:03.690
but especially with writing this book.

2349
01:39:03.690 --> 01:39:06.510
I mean, I was sitting eight hours at a stretch sometimes

2350
01:39:06.510 --> 01:39:10.200
on my computer looking out through the glass at the woods,

2351
01:39:10.200 --> 01:39:11.729
and, you know, I tell you,

2352
01:39:11.729 --> 01:39:16.729
like, getting outside, you feel it, you feel better.

2353
01:39:17.400 --> 01:39:22.260
It just has this, you know, exercising outside,

2354
01:39:22.260 --> 01:39:23.700
I mean, I love the gym I go to,

2355
01:39:23.700 --> 01:39:26.580
I enjoy hopping on my Peloton bike,

2356
01:39:26.580 --> 01:39:27.630
but there's something about

2357
01:39:27.630 --> 01:39:29.850
being outside in the woods in nature

2358
01:39:29.850 --> 01:39:33.160
that feels very different for mood, everything else,

2359
01:39:33.160 --> 01:39:35.370
and it turns out that that effect is very real,

2360
01:39:35.370 --> 01:39:40.370
this germicidal constituent, and it can be tricky.

2361
01:39:40.500 --> 01:39:42.090
It was tricky in the beginning of the pandemic

2362
01:39:42.090 --> 01:39:43.642
when everybody was being told to stay inside,

2363
01:39:43.642 --> 01:39:45.570
and isolate, and all of that,

2364
01:39:45.570 --> 01:39:47.490
but we were lucky in Washington

2365
01:39:47.490 --> 01:39:49.890
to have these beautiful outdoor spaces,

2366
01:39:49.890 --> 01:39:52.520
and you're lucky here too in California,

2367
01:39:52.520 --> 01:39:55.590
but that's something that people really should think about

2368
01:39:55.590 --> 01:39:58.650
incorporating, is this time outside in nature,

2369
01:39:58.650 --> 01:40:00.244
and ideally getting a little dirty,

2370
01:40:00.244 --> 01:40:02.370
exposure to soil microbes.

2371
01:40:02.370 --> 01:40:06.570
Yeah, it's not just the air and the sunlight,

2372
01:40:06.570 --> 01:40:11.570
it's the greater microbiome of our external environments

2373
01:40:11.615 --> 01:40:14.340
and the diversity of, you know,

2374
01:40:14.340 --> 01:40:17.740
whatever is growing in the air that we're breathing,

2375
01:40:17.740 --> 01:40:21.750
that is serving our gut health.

2376
01:40:21.750 --> 01:40:22.800
I had a funny story.

2377
01:40:22.800 --> 01:40:26.740
I had COVID in January, but I had read that study

2378
01:40:26.740 --> 01:40:30.157
a year before, and I had told my family,

2379
01:40:30.157 --> 01:40:32.377
"If I get COVID, you need to put a little cot

2380
01:40:32.377 --> 01:40:34.807
"outside on the deck for me,

2381
01:40:34.807 --> 01:40:36.300
"and that's where I'm gonna isolate,"

2382
01:40:36.300 --> 01:40:37.530
and just bring my food out there,

2383
01:40:37.530 --> 01:40:40.110
and that's where I want to recover.

2384
01:40:40.110 --> 01:40:42.890
I had COVID in January, a couple days after New Year's.

2385
01:40:42.890 --> 01:40:45.090
That's not ideal.

2386
01:40:45.090 --> 01:40:48.417
The day before a huge snowstorm,

2387
01:40:48.417 --> 01:40:51.000
and I'm somebody who, you know, as I said, I like heat,

2388
01:40:51.000 --> 01:40:52.340
I like it hot.

2389
01:40:52.340 --> 01:40:55.710
So, my family was like, "Yeah, about that cot."

2390
01:40:55.710 --> 01:40:56.790
I was like, "Yeah,"

2391
01:40:56.790 --> 01:40:57.927
I mean, it turns out I had it

2392
01:40:57.927 --> 01:40:59.160
and my husband had it the next day.

2393
01:40:59.160 --> 01:41:01.170
My daughters were all able to isolate together,

2394
01:41:01.170 --> 01:41:02.700
which was marvelous,

2395
01:41:02.700 --> 01:41:05.460
but what I did do, beginning with that next day

2396
01:41:05.460 --> 01:41:08.160
with a huge snowstorm, was I was out.

2397
01:41:08.160 --> 01:41:09.449
I would bundle up, double mask,

2398
01:41:09.449 --> 01:41:12.240
and I would hit the road late at night,

2399
01:41:12.240 --> 01:41:15.180
and I was, you know, trying to do as many miles as I could,

2400
01:41:15.180 --> 01:41:17.700
breathing, because one of the things I also experienced was,

2401
01:41:17.700 --> 01:41:22.700
lying on my back, I could feel alveoli tissue collapsing.

2402
01:41:23.130 --> 01:41:24.545
It was like, I can't lie on my back

2403
01:41:24.545 --> 01:41:26.100
all day like this in the bed.

2404
01:41:26.100 --> 01:41:29.400
I could feel it, and starting to get a little chest pain.

2405
01:41:29.400 --> 01:41:31.179
So, I got an incentive spirometer,

2406
01:41:31.179 --> 01:41:33.690
one of those things they use in the hospital

2407
01:41:33.690 --> 01:41:36.240
after you've had surgery, where you do the deep breaths.

2408
01:41:36.240 --> 01:41:37.590
So, I was doing my incentive spirometer.

2409
01:41:37.590 --> 01:41:38.670
I was going out and walking.

2410
01:41:38.670 --> 01:41:40.470
I mean, I wasn't out trying to run a marathon,

2411
01:41:40.470 --> 01:41:44.400
but really doing as much as I felt able to expand the lungs,

2412
01:41:44.400 --> 01:41:46.860
and some of those things, those basic things, you know,

2413
01:41:46.860 --> 01:41:49.190
we put people in the hospital on their backs,

2414
01:41:49.190 --> 01:41:51.060
bad things happen.

2415
01:41:51.060 --> 01:41:53.130
So, some of these things that people can think about,

2416
01:41:53.130 --> 01:41:55.770
you know, if you're able, and again, not to overdo it,

2417
01:41:55.770 --> 01:41:56.970
but do what you can manage,

2418
01:41:56.970 --> 01:41:59.759
which will depend on your baseline of getting outside,

2419
01:41:59.759 --> 01:42:02.700
fresh air, breathing deeply, all of these things.

2420
01:42:02.700 --> 01:42:03.723
They're important.

2421
01:42:04.740 --> 01:42:06.570
From a policy perspective,

2422
01:42:06.570 --> 01:42:11.343
I presume that the powers that be at the CDC, et cetera,

2423
01:42:12.210 --> 01:42:16.920
had some awareness of this 1918 Spanish flu epidemic,

2424
01:42:16.920 --> 01:42:18.990
and the difference between recuperating

2425
01:42:18.990 --> 01:42:21.240
outside versus inside.

2426
01:42:21.240 --> 01:42:24.330
Why and how does that not get translated

2427
01:42:24.330 --> 01:42:27.660
into public messaging about

2428
01:42:27.660 --> 01:42:29.250
how to best take care of ourselves?

2429
01:42:29.250 --> 01:42:30.930
We were told to stay inside.

2430
01:42:30.930 --> 01:42:33.393
I live out in the middle of nowhere,

2431
01:42:34.590 --> 01:42:36.960
and habitually run trails where,

2432
01:42:36.960 --> 01:42:39.540
if I'm out for a couple hours, maybe I see two people,

2433
01:42:39.540 --> 01:42:42.130
and there was yellow tape, like, on these crazy trails.

2434
01:42:42.130 --> 01:42:43.410
On the playgrounds.

2435
01:42:43.410 --> 01:42:45.780
It was basically illegal to go out

2436
01:42:45.780 --> 01:42:48.422
and do exactly this thing that has been proven

2437
01:42:48.422 --> 01:42:53.422
to be beneficial, as opposed to harmful as we were told.

2438
01:42:54.690 --> 01:42:57.410
The lack of public health messaging around this

2439
01:42:57.410 --> 01:42:59.160
is really astounding.

2440
01:42:59.160 --> 01:43:01.290
If you look at something like antibiotics,

2441
01:43:01.290 --> 01:43:04.530
we clearly, if you take rotavirus infection,

2442
01:43:04.530 --> 01:43:07.182
rotavirus viral infection kills about

2443
01:43:07.182 --> 01:43:11.580
half a million children a year around the world,

2444
01:43:11.580 --> 01:43:13.353
serious diarrheal illness.

2445
01:43:14.190 --> 01:43:18.199
We know that if you take certain proteins from bacteria

2446
01:43:18.199 --> 01:43:21.140
and inject it into mice with rotavirus,

2447
01:43:21.140 --> 01:43:25.440
it halts the diarrhea, and basically they're good,

2448
01:43:25.440 --> 01:43:29.334
and we know that kids who have been on an antibiotic

2449
01:43:29.334 --> 01:43:32.220
immediately before getting a rotavirus viral infection,

2450
01:43:32.220 --> 01:43:33.660
and, by the way, antibiotic as you know,

2451
01:43:33.660 --> 01:43:36.270
has no activity against a viral infection,

2452
01:43:36.270 --> 01:43:38.790
but they still sometimes, "Oh, diarrhea, they're sick,"

2453
01:43:38.790 --> 01:43:40.230
they get this antibiotic.

2454
01:43:40.230 --> 01:43:42.810
We know that they're five times more likely

2455
01:43:42.810 --> 01:43:45.960
to have a bad outcome from rotavirus.

2456
01:43:45.960 --> 01:43:48.630
We know the same thing for COVID and other viral illnesses.

2457
01:43:48.630 --> 01:43:50.070
Gut bacteria are essential.

2458
01:43:50.070 --> 01:43:54.780
They're an essential part of fighting these viral illnesses,

2459
01:43:54.780 --> 01:43:56.520
not just with modulating the immune system,

2460
01:43:56.520 --> 01:43:58.821
but by actually releasing proteins that help

2461
01:43:58.821 --> 01:44:01.680
by competing for binding sites with the viruses,

2462
01:44:01.680 --> 01:44:03.030
all these different things.

2463
01:44:03.930 --> 01:44:06.112
No messaging, nothing saying,

2464
01:44:06.112 --> 01:44:09.130
don't take an antibiotic if you have a viral infection,

2465
01:44:09.130 --> 01:44:11.190
and not just no messaging.

2466
01:44:11.190 --> 01:44:13.440
We still see physicians, hospitals,

2467
01:44:13.440 --> 01:44:14.348
other healthcare practitioners saying,

2468
01:44:14.348 --> 01:44:15.967
"Well, you have COVID,

2469
01:44:15.967 --> 01:44:17.850
"but just in case," and granted,

2470
01:44:17.850 --> 01:44:20.370
they're definitely--
Prescribing antibiotics.

2471
01:44:20.370 --> 01:44:21.330
You have bacterial--

2472
01:44:21.330 --> 01:44:23.040
I thought we'd kind of gotten past that.

2473
01:44:23.040 --> 01:44:24.240
It's been astounding, Rich,

2474
01:44:24.240 --> 01:44:27.090
and, of course, you have to pull out from that

2475
01:44:27.090 --> 01:44:28.320
the people who either have

2476
01:44:28.320 --> 01:44:31.530
a secondary bacterial super infection or are at risk,

2477
01:44:31.530 --> 01:44:32.790
but the majority of people

2478
01:44:32.790 --> 01:44:37.050
who were getting slapped on antibiotics had none of that

2479
01:44:37.050 --> 01:44:39.844
and actually worsening this person's

2480
01:44:39.844 --> 01:44:42.690
likely prognosis with antibiotics.

2481
01:44:42.690 --> 01:44:45.210
So, things like getting outside things like, you know,

2482
01:44:45.210 --> 01:44:47.220
avoiding unnecessary antibiotics,

2483
01:44:47.220 --> 01:44:48.910
things like, don't suppress your cough or fever.

2484
01:44:48.910 --> 01:44:53.152
I mean, these basic things, and it's puzzling to me,

2485
01:44:53.152 --> 01:44:55.830
and I don't know if maybe the feeling is,

2486
01:44:55.830 --> 01:44:57.420
if we focus on these things,

2487
01:44:57.420 --> 01:44:59.746
it will seem like we're detracting from those,

2488
01:44:59.746 --> 01:45:02.246
but that that doesn't really make sense.

2489
01:45:02.246 --> 01:45:06.493
Yeah, I feel like, you know, they should have trusted us

2490
01:45:06.493 --> 01:45:09.420
a little bit more to understand that,

2491
01:45:09.420 --> 01:45:10.285
yes, we need both.

2492
01:45:10.285 --> 01:45:12.060
We need to take care of ourselves

2493
01:45:12.060 --> 01:45:13.800
to put ourselves in the best position

2494
01:45:13.800 --> 01:45:18.553
to, you know, basically fend off this illness

2495
01:45:18.553 --> 01:45:22.350
that we're almost undoubtedly gonna come into contact with,

2496
01:45:22.350 --> 01:45:25.200
and understanding that not everybody is equal

2497
01:45:25.200 --> 01:45:26.970
and there are certain things that we can do

2498
01:45:26.970 --> 01:45:28.833
to put us in a better position,

2499
01:45:28.833 --> 01:45:30.900
you know, if and when that occurs,

2500
01:45:30.900 --> 01:45:32.733
and it's not an if, it's a when,

2501
01:45:33.690 --> 01:45:35.395
but I don't know, maybe they felt like

2502
01:45:35.395 --> 01:45:38.850
that would detract from the other messaging,

2503
01:45:38.850 --> 01:45:42.630
but I think we're gonna be doing a forensic excavation

2504
01:45:42.630 --> 01:45:45.570
of what went wrong and what went right

2505
01:45:45.570 --> 01:45:46.980
for many years to come,

2506
01:45:46.980 --> 01:45:50.943
and, because we will inevitably face another pandemic,

2507
01:45:52.320 --> 01:45:55.020
my hope is that we'll have our ducks in a row

2508
01:45:55.020 --> 01:45:57.030
a little bit better for next time.

2509
01:45:57.030 --> 01:45:59.250
Well, if you look at the statistics,

2510
01:45:59.250 --> 01:46:03.330
there've been over 30 different viruses in the last 50 years

2511
01:46:03.330 --> 01:46:05.280
for which there is no cure or real treatment.

2512
01:46:05.280 --> 01:46:10.003
So, things like hepatitis C, HIV, Ebola, SARS-CoV-2,

2513
01:46:10.003 --> 01:46:13.110
a study from Duke in 2021 looked at

2514
01:46:13.110 --> 01:46:15.739
the statistical likelihood of a pandemic

2515
01:46:15.739 --> 01:46:19.800
of the proportions we've seen with COVID happening,

2516
01:46:19.800 --> 01:46:22.630
and calculated that at 2% per year,

2517
01:46:22.630 --> 01:46:26.770
which means for somebody born in 2000,

2518
01:46:26.770 --> 01:46:30.840
that's a 40% likelihood by 2020 that this would've happened,

2519
01:46:30.840 --> 01:46:34.950
but, yet, we still seem very surprised, you know, and again,

2520
01:46:34.950 --> 01:46:37.230
there's precedent for all of this,

2521
01:46:37.230 --> 01:46:39.930
and a lot of the things that we did

2522
01:46:39.930 --> 01:46:44.340
seem that they weren't super innovative,

2523
01:46:44.340 --> 01:46:45.767
and I think it's really easy to look back

2524
01:46:45.767 --> 01:46:47.310
with a retrospective scope

2525
01:46:47.310 --> 01:46:49.170
and say, "We didn't do this right, we didn't do that right,"

2526
01:46:49.170 --> 01:46:51.780
and there was clearly a lot of panic.

2527
01:46:51.780 --> 01:46:54.216
I feel like we're in a less panicked mode right now

2528
01:46:54.216 --> 01:46:56.150
and we're able to be more thoughtful,

2529
01:46:56.150 --> 01:47:00.268
thoughtful about maybe stratifying people based on risk

2530
01:47:00.268 --> 01:47:03.210
and who needs to do what based on risks

2531
01:47:03.210 --> 01:47:07.060
like age, and comorbidities, and medication, et cetera,

2532
01:47:07.060 --> 01:47:08.700
and that's been good to see,

2533
01:47:08.700 --> 01:47:10.522
and also who needs to test and who doesn't,

2534
01:47:10.522 --> 01:47:13.080
and who needs to get boosted and who doesn't.

2535
01:47:13.080 --> 01:47:13.989
So, I think we're seeing

2536
01:47:13.989 --> 01:47:16.132
a lot more thoughtfulness around that

2537
01:47:16.132 --> 01:47:18.900
and hopefully we will be better prepared.

2538
01:47:18.900 --> 01:47:20.460
What this has really shown us is

2539
01:47:20.460 --> 01:47:22.770
that our public health system really

2540
01:47:22.770 --> 01:47:24.160
was not what it needed to be.

2541
01:47:24.160 --> 01:47:28.226
Right, so, hopefully everybody's re-watching the game tape

2542
01:47:28.226 --> 01:47:30.087
and trying to learn, you know,

2543
01:47:30.087 --> 01:47:31.980
how to do it better next time,

2544
01:47:31.980 --> 01:47:32.813
because we're gonna need that.

2545
01:47:32.813 --> 01:47:34.770
I feel like it was a trial run.

2546
01:47:34.770 --> 01:47:36.660
Listen, it could have been a lot worse,

2547
01:47:36.660 --> 01:47:37.740
but a lot of people died.

2548
01:47:37.740 --> 01:47:38.573
It wasn't nothing.

2549
01:47:38.573 --> 01:47:41.820
It was a very significant event in all of our lives,

2550
01:47:41.820 --> 01:47:43.447
and plenty of people perished,

2551
01:47:43.447 --> 01:47:46.800
and it's incumbent upon us to sit down

2552
01:47:46.800 --> 01:47:49.110
and figure out how to do better next time,

2553
01:47:49.110 --> 01:47:52.075
and my sense is that smart people are doing that.

2554
01:47:52.075 --> 01:47:53.033
I hope they are.

2555
01:47:53.033 --> 01:47:54.455
And it's not over,

2556
01:47:54.455 --> 01:47:56.580
but at the same time.
Yeah, that's the thing,

2557
01:47:56.580 --> 01:47:57.730
I'm acting like, yeah, so.

2558
01:47:57.730 --> 01:48:02.730
We also saw incredible generosity and graciousness,

2559
01:48:04.230 --> 01:48:09.035
and community in a way I don't think I've seen before,

2560
01:48:09.035 --> 01:48:11.880
and that I can remember.

2561
01:48:11.880 --> 01:48:13.410
So, we saw some good things too.

2562
01:48:13.410 --> 01:48:14.243
Yeah.

2563
01:48:15.630 --> 01:48:20.233
You mentioned the prescription of antibiotics,

2564
01:48:21.480 --> 01:48:25.008
and, you know, kind of the misguidedness of that

2565
01:48:25.008 --> 01:48:26.470
in the midst of all of this.

2566
01:48:26.470 --> 01:48:28.710
What about Paxlovid?

2567
01:48:28.710 --> 01:48:29.580
Like, that's something that's

2568
01:48:29.580 --> 01:48:32.670
being prescribed quite a bit for COVID.

2569
01:48:32.670 --> 01:48:35.430
Is there a sense of the efficacy of that?

2570
01:48:35.430 --> 01:48:38.835
Is there a sense of the downstream gut health

2571
01:48:38.835 --> 01:48:40.794
and immune system implications

2572
01:48:40.794 --> 01:48:43.530
of doing a five day course of that?

2573
01:48:43.530 --> 01:48:45.000
I think we don't really know yet

2574
01:48:45.000 --> 01:48:48.840
in the sort of real life data what the downside is,

2575
01:48:48.840 --> 01:48:52.950
but what I will say is, I think it's being prescribed a lot

2576
01:48:52.950 --> 01:48:55.800
outside of the guidelines for who should get it

2577
01:48:55.800 --> 01:48:57.401
in terms of age, or somebody

2578
01:48:57.401 --> 01:49:00.750
who has a comorbidity that qualifies,

2579
01:49:00.750 --> 01:49:03.600
and I think part of that is,

2580
01:49:03.600 --> 01:49:06.224
it's pressure on the physician side to feel like

2581
01:49:06.224 --> 01:49:08.190
I need to be able to do something other than

2582
01:49:08.190 --> 01:49:11.505
to tell you to go home, and walk a little bit outside,

2583
01:49:11.505 --> 01:49:13.770
and eat some vegetables,

2584
01:49:13.770 --> 01:49:16.305
So, physicians want to feel like

2585
01:49:16.305 --> 01:49:19.460
we can do something actively,

2586
01:49:19.460 --> 01:49:21.600
and there's also pressure on the consumer side

2587
01:49:21.600 --> 01:49:22.740
is that people want to know.

2588
01:49:22.740 --> 01:49:25.766
They're like, "Look, we live in this modern world

2589
01:49:25.766 --> 01:49:27.807
"where we can take a heart from somebody

2590
01:49:27.807 --> 01:49:29.167
"and put it in somebody else,

2591
01:49:29.167 --> 01:49:30.217
"and do all of these things,

2592
01:49:30.217 --> 01:49:32.137
"like, you must be able to do something for me,"

2593
01:49:32.137 --> 01:49:34.745
and so, I think there's pressure on both sides

2594
01:49:34.745 --> 01:49:39.363
and there's clearly efficacy in certain groups of people,

2595
01:49:40.230 --> 01:49:42.195
but I think it is being over-prescribed

2596
01:49:42.195 --> 01:49:44.190
and outside of the guidelines,

2597
01:49:44.190 --> 01:49:46.980
and I think we'll have some data coming in soon.

2598
01:49:46.980 --> 01:49:48.090
You know, for things like this,

2599
01:49:48.090 --> 01:49:51.300
a short exposure probably isn't going to be problematic,

2600
01:49:51.300 --> 01:49:53.077
but it's when we see people on longer courses

2601
01:49:53.077 --> 01:49:54.760
or repeated courses,

2602
01:49:54.760 --> 01:49:57.300
we'll start to have some data in terms of, you know,

2603
01:49:57.300 --> 01:49:59.950
what the potential downside there is.

2604
01:49:59.950 --> 01:50:04.420
Last time we spoke, it was a lot about how to live dirty,

2605
01:50:04.420 --> 01:50:07.530
eat clean, and I have to ask, like,

2606
01:50:07.530 --> 01:50:11.890
has there been any new revelation since we last spoke about

2607
01:50:14.010 --> 01:50:15.990
the consumer products that we use?

2608
01:50:15.990 --> 01:50:17.940
We've been talking about food, and pharmaceuticals,

2609
01:50:17.940 --> 01:50:20.220
and the like, but what about beauty products?

2610
01:50:20.220 --> 01:50:24.225
What about, you know, our soaps and the way in which we,

2611
01:50:24.225 --> 01:50:28.230
you know, conduct our hygiene, and the clothes that we wear,

2612
01:50:28.230 --> 01:50:30.156
and the dyes that those clothes are made with?

2613
01:50:30.156 --> 01:50:35.156
Like, how are you understanding our habits around this

2614
01:50:35.160 --> 01:50:36.725
and some things that we should be thinking about?

2615
01:50:36.725 --> 01:50:38.670
Since we last convened, Rich?

2616
01:50:38.670 --> 01:50:40.208
Which, goodness, it's been seven years.

2617
01:50:40.208 --> 01:50:42.190
That's a long time.

2618
01:50:42.190 --> 01:50:45.780
What's been exciting is to see people realizing

2619
01:50:45.780 --> 01:50:47.730
like, "Oh, we were too clean,"

2620
01:50:47.730 --> 01:50:49.140
but what's also interesting is

2621
01:50:49.140 --> 01:50:50.370
this idea that now you just

2622
01:50:50.370 --> 01:50:51.990
have to put a probiotic in something, you know,

2623
01:50:51.990 --> 01:50:54.314
probiotic shampoo, and probiotic, you know?

2624
01:50:54.314 --> 01:50:55.687
So, we've gone from,

2625
01:50:55.687 --> 01:50:59.070
"Oh, the anti-bacterial, to now the pro-bacterial,"

2626
01:50:59.070 --> 01:51:02.348
and the thing that I want people to remember is

2627
01:51:02.348 --> 01:51:05.130
that it's less about adding something

2628
01:51:05.130 --> 01:51:09.390
and more about not doing something that is harmful.

2629
01:51:09.390 --> 01:51:14.390
So, thinking about what practices can we stop or decrease

2630
01:51:14.590 --> 01:51:17.550
that are removing our healthy microbes,

2631
01:51:17.550 --> 01:51:19.620
as opposed to, "No, I'm gonna add a microbe to something."

2632
01:51:19.620 --> 01:51:21.950
So, just not washing as much is super helpful.

2633
01:51:21.950 --> 01:51:24.541
I know I have rosacea, super sensitive skin,

2634
01:51:24.541 --> 01:51:26.166
it's under, well, good control now.

2635
01:51:26.166 --> 01:51:29.100
It's not flared up in years, and I credit part of that

2636
01:51:29.100 --> 01:51:32.100
to the fact that I don't wash my face that much,

2637
01:51:32.100 --> 01:51:34.573
and that really helps, and the same thing, you know,

2638
01:51:34.573 --> 01:51:36.774
I have people who are suffering from hair loss,

2639
01:51:36.774 --> 01:51:38.887
and I'm like, "Why are you putting

2640
01:51:38.887 --> 01:51:40.080
"all this stuff in your hair?"

2641
01:51:40.080 --> 01:51:41.440
Like, you've gotta give your scalp

2642
01:51:41.440 --> 01:51:44.940
and the scalp microbes a little chance to regenerate,

2643
01:51:44.940 --> 01:51:48.240
and so, I think because we are so product driven,

2644
01:51:48.240 --> 01:51:51.780
we want to take something, and to do something,

2645
01:51:51.780 --> 01:51:54.770
and I'm happy to see that we're having, you know,

2646
01:51:54.770 --> 01:51:59.442
actually some pretty terrific pro-bacterial products

2647
01:51:59.442 --> 01:52:01.650
compared to all the antibacterial, you know,

2648
01:52:01.650 --> 01:52:04.740
this idea that we're gonna cleanse everything away.

2649
01:52:04.740 --> 01:52:06.840
We've definitely moved away from that,

2650
01:52:06.840 --> 01:52:10.850
and you look at products like some seaweed based products,

2651
01:52:10.850 --> 01:52:13.626
a company like OSEA that's based here in Malibu

2652
01:52:13.626 --> 01:52:16.230
that I think makes fantastic products.

2653
01:52:16.230 --> 01:52:17.877
So, we're seeing much more of that,

2654
01:52:17.877 --> 01:52:20.610
and I think the barrier for entry is lower

2655
01:52:20.610 --> 01:52:23.400
because it's easier for somebody to put a product out now.

2656
01:52:23.400 --> 01:52:26.610
You know, with platforms, you don't have to go through

2657
01:52:26.610 --> 01:52:29.579
two or three of the large CPG companies to do that.

2658
01:52:29.579 --> 01:52:32.190
So, we're seeing a lot of innovation,

2659
01:52:32.190 --> 01:52:35.550
but I think the focus still should be on, again,

2660
01:52:35.550 --> 01:52:37.710
your body is producing this stuff.

2661
01:52:37.710 --> 01:52:38.790
If you think about stuff

2662
01:52:38.790 --> 01:52:40.950
like the oil on your skin and in your hair,

2663
01:52:40.950 --> 01:52:43.140
your body is producing the stuff

2664
01:52:43.140 --> 01:52:47.340
that is custom produced for your body's pH,

2665
01:52:47.340 --> 01:52:50.700
and your ideal skin type, and what do we do?

2666
01:52:50.700 --> 01:52:51.900
We scrub that away,

2667
01:52:51.900 --> 01:52:53.807
and then we put some store bought version on,

2668
01:52:53.807 --> 01:52:56.760
and so, you know, we have to think about

2669
01:52:56.760 --> 01:53:00.630
how we can work in concert a little bit more

2670
01:53:00.630 --> 01:53:02.940
with what our body is already doing,

2671
01:53:02.940 --> 01:53:03.963
and sort of, enhancing it,

2672
01:53:03.963 --> 01:53:06.440
but not destroying, and then replacing.

2673
01:53:06.440 --> 01:53:09.480
Right, it's analogous to the cough suppressant.

2674
01:53:09.480 --> 01:53:12.090
If we're just putting soap on our face all the time

2675
01:53:12.090 --> 01:53:14.430
and removing all of those oils,

2676
01:53:14.430 --> 01:53:16.740
those oils are there for a reason,

2677
01:53:16.740 --> 01:53:19.053
like, why are we getting rid of them?

2678
01:53:20.100 --> 01:53:23.370
It seems like a good rule of thumb, although, you know,

2679
01:53:23.370 --> 01:53:26.013
orthogonal to kind of our current modern day habits

2680
01:53:26.013 --> 01:53:27.960
around some--
And my sister likes

2681
01:53:27.960 --> 01:53:30.110
to point out, because I've been quoted about, you know,

2682
01:53:30.110 --> 01:53:32.385
not bathing too much and stuff, and she likes to point out,

2683
01:53:32.385 --> 01:53:35.280
like, she's like, "That's not everybody in the family."

2684
01:53:35.280 --> 01:53:38.790
I like to bathe every day, and sometimes I'll go over there,

2685
01:53:38.790 --> 01:53:40.350
and she lives nearby, and I would've,

2686
01:53:40.350 --> 01:53:41.760
like, gone for a long run in the woods,

2687
01:53:41.760 --> 01:53:43.610
and maybe done some yoga, and I'm like,

2688
01:53:43.610 --> 01:53:44.820
"I don't smell bad, right?:

2689
01:53:44.820 --> 01:53:46.267
And she's like, "Yeah, you don't smell

2690
01:53:46.267 --> 01:53:47.621
"as good as you think you do."

2691
01:53:47.621 --> 01:53:48.750
(laughing)

2692
01:53:48.750 --> 01:53:49.590
It is interesting,

2693
01:53:49.590 --> 01:53:52.200
there are so many companies and new products

2694
01:53:52.200 --> 01:53:54.000
that are coming out that are, you know,

2695
01:53:54.000 --> 01:53:56.740
sort of chemical free or more natural,

2696
01:53:56.740 --> 01:53:58.087
and I think there's a sense like,

2697
01:53:58.087 --> 01:53:59.730
"Oh, more natural is better,"

2698
01:53:59.730 --> 01:54:01.710
but it also has to be efficacious.

2699
01:54:01.710 --> 01:54:03.450
Some of these products don't actually work.

2700
01:54:03.450 --> 01:54:06.030
If they don't work, we're not gonna use them,

2701
01:54:06.030 --> 01:54:09.510
but in the pecking order of, you know,

2702
01:54:09.510 --> 01:54:11.750
like, there's the dirty dozen.

2703
01:54:11.750 --> 01:54:13.110
What are the organic foods?

2704
01:54:13.110 --> 01:54:16.248
So, in consumer products like chlorinated water,

2705
01:54:16.248 --> 01:54:19.860
or fluoride in our toothpaste, or certain kind of shampoos,

2706
01:54:19.860 --> 01:54:21.739
do you have a sense of the most important things

2707
01:54:21.739 --> 01:54:24.280
that we should be paying attention to or getting rid of?

2708
01:54:24.280 --> 01:54:26.760
Yeah, the really sudsy things

2709
01:54:26.760 --> 01:54:29.790
like the sodium sulfate is a problem

2710
01:54:29.790 --> 01:54:32.190
and people like that, because they like a sud,

2711
01:54:32.190 --> 01:54:33.750
but some of those things,

2712
01:54:33.750 --> 01:54:36.540
what they actually do is they disrupt the skin,

2713
01:54:36.540 --> 01:54:38.520
the integrity of the skin, and they make it easier

2714
01:54:38.520 --> 01:54:41.520
for pathogenic bacteria to penetrate,

2715
01:54:41.520 --> 01:54:43.660
or they're really toxic to the, you know,

2716
01:54:43.660 --> 01:54:46.488
the population of staph or corona bacterium

2717
01:54:46.488 --> 01:54:48.120
that live on the skin,

2718
01:54:48.120 --> 01:54:51.330
that are part of the skin's important ecosystem,

2719
01:54:51.330 --> 01:54:53.640
and certainly things that have a lot of alcohol in them,

2720
01:54:53.640 --> 01:54:55.800
because remember, before we had antibiotics,

2721
01:54:55.800 --> 01:54:57.487
we had alcohol as our main antibacterial,

2722
01:54:57.487 --> 01:54:59.398
and even now, if you go to get blood drawn,

2723
01:54:59.398 --> 01:55:02.250
they put an alcohol swab to clean the skin.

2724
01:55:02.250 --> 01:55:05.220
So, products that have a lot of alcohol in them

2725
01:55:05.220 --> 01:55:10.220
are also potentially problematic for those fragile microbes.

2726
01:55:12.120 --> 01:55:14.850
I want to touch on long COVID for a minute.

2727
01:55:14.850 --> 01:55:16.620
I feel like this has become, like, a COVID...

2728
01:55:16.620 --> 01:55:20.820
This is not meant to be, like, a COVID focused conversation,

2729
01:55:20.820 --> 01:55:22.515
but because COVID is the virus

2730
01:55:22.515 --> 01:55:24.030
that we're currently contending with,

2731
01:55:24.030 --> 01:55:27.960
it feels appropriate to use that as a lens

2732
01:55:27.960 --> 01:55:29.367
to talk about these other things

2733
01:55:29.367 --> 01:55:31.320
that you talk about in the book.

2734
01:55:31.320 --> 01:55:32.970
Long COVID is a tricky one.

2735
01:55:32.970 --> 01:55:34.980
I feel like we don't really know

2736
01:55:34.980 --> 01:55:36.025
what we're dealing with here,

2737
01:55:36.025 --> 01:55:38.760
and you have some interesting information

2738
01:55:38.760 --> 01:55:40.470
in the book about this,

2739
01:55:40.470 --> 01:55:43.260
about what we know, and what we don't know know,

2740
01:55:43.260 --> 01:55:45.420
and the relationship of gut health

2741
01:55:45.420 --> 01:55:48.933
on this "thing" we're calling long COVID.

2742
01:55:50.280 --> 01:55:52.440
One of the things with the book,

2743
01:55:52.440 --> 01:55:56.070
I purposefully did not put COVID in the title.

2744
01:55:56.070 --> 01:55:56.903
The book is called

2745
01:55:56.903 --> 01:55:58.687
"the Anti-Viral Gut: Tackling Pathogens

2746
01:55:58.687 --> 01:55:59.880
"from the Inside Out,"

2747
01:55:59.880 --> 01:56:04.880
and while COVID is definitely on the radar,

2748
01:56:05.010 --> 01:56:07.440
there's a lot in the book as, you know,

2749
01:56:07.440 --> 01:56:10.590
you've read it, about other viruses, about polio,

2750
01:56:10.590 --> 01:56:12.300
about influenza, et cetera,

2751
01:56:12.300 --> 01:56:14.256
and when it comes to long COVID,

2752
01:56:14.256 --> 01:56:16.321
or postacute sequelae COVID,

2753
01:56:16.321 --> 01:56:19.383
or long haul, or all the different names we have for it,

2754
01:56:20.850 --> 01:56:22.940
one of the things we know a lot about

2755
01:56:22.940 --> 01:56:23.958
is postviral syndromes.

2756
01:56:23.958 --> 01:56:26.190
This is not the first postviral syndrome,

2757
01:56:26.190 --> 01:56:27.025
it won't be the last.

2758
01:56:27.025 --> 01:56:29.940
I think the problem, Rich, is that we weren't expecting it

2759
01:56:29.940 --> 01:56:30.900
because we were thinking

2760
01:56:30.900 --> 01:56:32.790
this is kind of like a bad flu virus,

2761
01:56:32.790 --> 01:56:35.300
and postviral syndromes from flu are not as common,

2762
01:56:35.300 --> 01:56:36.900
but think about AIDS.

2763
01:56:36.900 --> 01:56:40.230
AIDS is a chronic form of HIV.

2764
01:56:40.230 --> 01:56:43.012
HIV, the human immunodeficiency virus,

2765
01:56:43.012 --> 01:56:47.760
causes AIDS, and it causes us prolonged disease.

2766
01:56:47.760 --> 01:56:50.190
Now we have pretty good anti-retrovirals for that,

2767
01:56:50.190 --> 01:56:51.840
but it's still a chronic condition.

2768
01:56:51.840 --> 01:56:55.720
You think about hepatitis C can cause cirrhosis of the liver

2769
01:56:55.720 --> 01:56:57.880
and liver cancer in many patients.

2770
01:56:57.880 --> 01:57:00.540
You think about Epstein Barr virus causes mono,

2771
01:57:00.540 --> 01:57:02.950
it's been linked to chronic fatigue syndrome

2772
01:57:03.809 --> 01:57:08.809
and has been linked to MS now, multiple sclerosis,

2773
01:57:08.880 --> 01:57:10.925
and it's linked to certain forms of lymphoma,

2774
01:57:10.925 --> 01:57:12.750
so we have lots of precedent.

2775
01:57:12.750 --> 01:57:14.280
In my world, in gastroenterology,

2776
01:57:14.280 --> 01:57:15.120
we see something called

2777
01:57:15.120 --> 01:57:17.400
post-infectious irritable bowel syndrome,

2778
01:57:17.400 --> 01:57:18.860
post-infectious IBS,

2779
01:57:18.860 --> 01:57:23.860
which is people who have had a clear infectious episode

2780
01:57:24.252 --> 01:57:26.040
and then, lo and behold, you know,

2781
01:57:26.040 --> 01:57:27.867
maybe they have acute symptoms, diarrhea, or something,

2782
01:57:27.867 --> 01:57:30.780
nausea, vomiting, and those acute symptoms resolve,

2783
01:57:30.780 --> 01:57:31.680
but then, lo and behold,

2784
01:57:31.680 --> 01:57:32.648
they have irritable bowel syndrome

2785
01:57:32.648 --> 01:57:35.370
and the GI tract is sort of never the same.

2786
01:57:35.370 --> 01:57:38.790
So, we have a lot of experience with, if you think about

2787
01:57:38.790 --> 01:57:40.250
some of the sexually transmitted diseases,

2788
01:57:40.250 --> 01:57:43.590
like herpes, and human papillomavirus,

2789
01:57:43.590 --> 01:57:45.750
so HSV, HPV, et cetera,

2790
01:57:45.750 --> 01:57:47.931
we know there are acute and chronic forms of those

2791
01:57:47.931 --> 01:57:50.490
and we also know similarly

2792
01:57:50.490 --> 01:57:53.130
who is at risk for those chronic forms,

2793
01:57:53.130 --> 01:57:55.380
and there are some outliers to that,

2794
01:57:55.380 --> 01:57:57.000
but we know, for example,

2795
01:57:57.000 --> 01:57:59.770
within women with sexually transmitted diseases,

2796
01:57:59.770 --> 01:58:03.210
that the population of healthy lactobacillus bacteria

2797
01:58:03.210 --> 01:58:07.380
in the vagina that produce acid to repel these viruses,

2798
01:58:07.380 --> 01:58:10.991
that having lower levels can make you more susceptible

2799
01:58:10.991 --> 01:58:15.720
to the acute process of after exposure becoming infected,

2800
01:58:15.720 --> 01:58:18.390
and also to the chronic aspects of that.

2801
01:58:18.390 --> 01:58:20.700
So, postviral syndromes are not new,

2802
01:58:20.700 --> 01:58:23.300
but because this is a relatively new virus,

2803
01:58:23.300 --> 01:58:25.200
we're getting familiar with

2804
01:58:25.200 --> 01:58:29.400
what the postviral landscape looks like with long COVID,

2805
01:58:29.400 --> 01:58:32.675
and there are now more than 200 different symptoms

2806
01:58:32.675 --> 01:58:35.910
that are associated, and, of course, you know,

2807
01:58:35.910 --> 01:58:37.740
a lot of this is reporting.

2808
01:58:37.740 --> 01:58:39.330
Everything sort of gets put in,

2809
01:58:39.330 --> 01:58:41.730
but we know what some of the more common symptoms are,

2810
01:58:41.730 --> 01:58:46.410
fatigue, respiratory symptoms, brain fog, et cetera,

2811
01:58:46.410 --> 01:58:48.420
and if we look at that,

2812
01:58:48.420 --> 01:58:50.867
what we see are clear microbial differences

2813
01:58:50.867 --> 01:58:52.227
in a lot of these patients,

2814
01:58:52.227 --> 01:58:56.250
and a lot of this, we know from the CFS/ME literature

2815
01:58:56.250 --> 01:58:57.470
with chronic fatigue syndrome,

2816
01:58:57.470 --> 01:59:02.370
that researchers at Cornell were able to look at

2817
01:59:02.370 --> 01:59:07.370
the microbiome and identify people who had ME/CFS based on

2818
01:59:09.860 --> 01:59:11.550
what was going on microbially,

2819
01:59:11.550 --> 01:59:13.320
and we've seen a lot of those microbial differences.

2820
01:59:13.320 --> 01:59:16.650
We don't have a clear cut microbial signature yet,

2821
01:59:16.650 --> 01:59:18.510
but we have some hints,

2822
01:59:18.510 --> 01:59:19.493
and there was a study that was published

2823
01:59:19.493 --> 01:59:21.104
in one of our GI journals.

2824
01:59:21.104 --> 01:59:23.940
They followed 106 patients with acute COVID.

2825
01:59:23.940 --> 01:59:28.257
They found a very high rate of chronic symptoms, about 76%,

2826
01:59:28.257 --> 01:59:30.450
and in that 76%,

2827
01:59:30.450 --> 01:59:33.870
they found significant microbial abnormalities

2828
01:59:33.870 --> 01:59:36.270
in the people, the majority of people

2829
01:59:36.270 --> 01:59:37.920
who went out to have chronic symptoms

2830
01:59:37.920 --> 01:59:39.402
versus a minority who recovered,

2831
01:59:39.402 --> 01:59:42.450
very distinct microbial patterns.

2832
01:59:42.450 --> 01:59:44.207
So, it's difficult to sort out

2833
01:59:44.207 --> 01:59:46.320
how much of that is cause versus effect

2834
01:59:46.320 --> 01:59:49.050
because the virus itself can induce dysbiosis.

2835
01:59:49.050 --> 01:59:50.067
This wasn't something we talked about,

2836
01:59:50.067 --> 01:59:54.750
but the binding of SARS-CoV-2 to those ACE2 receptors

2837
01:59:54.750 --> 01:59:58.250
can induce microbial changes, dysbiotic microbial changes,

2838
01:59:58.250 --> 02:00:00.190
and then of course dysbiosis itself,

2839
02:00:00.190 --> 02:00:04.290
as we've been talking about, is a risk factor for, you know,

2840
02:00:04.290 --> 02:00:08.313
having worse outcomes, so there are both sides to that.

2841
02:00:09.390 --> 02:00:11.790
We know that autoimmunity is an issue.

2842
02:00:11.790 --> 02:00:13.320
We see autoimmune markers,

2843
02:00:13.320 --> 02:00:15.240
many of those patients never had

2844
02:00:15.240 --> 02:00:16.591
an actual autoimmune disease before,

2845
02:00:16.591 --> 02:00:18.930
and they still don't have an autoimmune disease,

2846
02:00:18.930 --> 02:00:20.990
but it's almost like a precursor,

2847
02:00:20.990 --> 02:00:22.980
and we don't know what's gonna happen

2848
02:00:22.980 --> 02:00:25.151
with some of those patients with the positive ANAs,

2849
02:00:25.151 --> 02:00:28.923
and different non-specific markers of inflammation.

2850
02:00:29.940 --> 02:00:32.758
Are they going to progress to an autoimmune disease

2851
02:00:32.758 --> 02:00:36.225
or is that stage where they are, where they have

2852
02:00:36.225 --> 02:00:39.990
an autoimmune marker, but not an actual autoimmune disease,

2853
02:00:39.990 --> 02:00:44.100
a specific disease in and unto itself.

2854
02:00:44.100 --> 02:00:48.210
We see EBV plays a role also.

2855
02:00:48.210 --> 02:00:50.790
Yeah, that was the thing that jumped out to me.

2856
02:00:50.790 --> 02:00:52.770
There's a sense that it might be

2857
02:00:52.770 --> 02:00:55.410
a reactivation of Epstein Barr virus,

2858
02:00:55.410 --> 02:00:59.970
and I didn't realize that, like, a huge percentage of people

2859
02:00:59.970 --> 02:01:04.140
are harboring some, you know, amount of that virus.

2860
02:01:04.140 --> 02:01:05.370
The majority of the population,

2861
02:01:05.370 --> 02:01:09.090
and EBV is fascinating, the history, because if we look at,

2862
02:01:09.090 --> 02:01:14.090
100 years ago they called it idiopathic adenitis,

2863
02:01:14.990 --> 02:01:16.517
idiopathic meaning unknown,

2864
02:01:16.517 --> 02:01:21.010
adenitis, means the glands are inflamed,

2865
02:01:21.010 --> 02:01:24.810
and then it wasn't until researchers at Hopkins, you know,

2866
02:01:24.810 --> 02:01:28.960
a couple decades later linked this to mono

2867
02:01:28.960 --> 02:01:31.980
and then just recently this year

2868
02:01:31.980 --> 02:01:36.980
some groundbreaking breaking research linking EBV to MS,

2869
02:01:37.170 --> 02:01:40.110
and the lymphoma connection has been made a few decades ago.

2870
02:01:40.110 --> 02:01:43.650
So, it's still evolving, and the role that EBV,

2871
02:01:43.650 --> 02:01:48.090
and reactivation of EBV may play in long COVID is still,

2872
02:01:48.090 --> 02:01:49.770
you know, there's still a big question mark there,

2873
02:01:49.770 --> 02:01:51.120
but again, we have precedent

2874
02:01:51.120 --> 02:01:54.030
because we have lots of research, in the MS world,

2875
02:01:54.030 --> 02:01:55.500
in the lymphoma world, et cetera.

2876
02:01:55.500 --> 02:01:58.170
So, for people out there who are struggling, you know,

2877
02:01:58.170 --> 02:02:01.530
I want them to know that even though the virus is novel,

2878
02:02:01.530 --> 02:02:03.150
the science is not novel.

2879
02:02:03.150 --> 02:02:04.350
We have a lot of data,

2880
02:02:04.350 --> 02:02:06.837
we have a lot of researchers, a lot we do know,

2881
02:02:06.837 --> 02:02:09.213
and there are lots of people working on it.

2882
02:02:11.320 --> 02:02:14.100
The final thing I want to get into with you

2883
02:02:14.100 --> 02:02:15.990
before I let you go,

2884
02:02:15.990 --> 02:02:17.670
I've gotta know where we're at

2885
02:02:17.670 --> 02:02:20.565
with the fecal transplant clinics

2886
02:02:20.565 --> 02:02:22.953
that are supposed to be popping up everywhere.

2887
02:02:24.285 --> 02:02:29.285
When you hear about all of these gut dysbiosis illnesses

2888
02:02:33.570 --> 02:02:37.290
and the extent to which people like you

2889
02:02:37.290 --> 02:02:40.500
can look at the gut flora, see what's missing,

2890
02:02:40.500 --> 02:02:42.810
see what's proliferating, et cetera,

2891
02:02:42.810 --> 02:02:46.350
it seems to follow that a lot of this could be resolved

2892
02:02:46.350 --> 02:02:51.350
by simply culturing fecal transplants,

2893
02:02:51.780 --> 02:02:54.510
and inserting them in people, and resolving this issue.

2894
02:02:54.510 --> 02:02:56.228
So, explain why that is

2895
02:02:56.228 --> 02:03:00.030
too simplistic a solution to these problems.

2896
02:03:00.030 --> 02:03:01.500
If we look at some diseases,

2897
02:03:01.500 --> 02:03:03.600
so if we go back to the Clostridium difficile,

2898
02:03:03.600 --> 02:03:05.910
I was talking about Clostridium difficile, again,

2899
02:03:05.910 --> 02:03:09.810
a bacteria lurking in many hospitals,

2900
02:03:09.810 --> 02:03:12.690
some of us are colonized with it, but at low levels.

2901
02:03:12.690 --> 02:03:13.710
You take an antibiotic,

2902
02:03:13.710 --> 02:03:15.450
you kill off a lot of your healthy bacteria,

2903
02:03:15.450 --> 02:03:18.180
and now the C. diff that's either there

2904
02:03:18.180 --> 02:03:19.200
or that you've acquired

2905
02:03:19.200 --> 02:03:21.210
when you went to visit your grandpa in the hospital

2906
02:03:21.210 --> 02:03:24.270
or if you yourself were hospitalized, is now proliferating

2907
02:03:24.270 --> 02:03:25.912
and you have C. diff colitis,

2908
02:03:25.912 --> 02:03:28.083
and you have this severe diarrheal illness,

2909
02:03:28.083 --> 02:03:32.130
a stool transplant in that setting,

2910
02:03:32.130 --> 02:03:33.210
in the study published

2911
02:03:33.210 --> 02:03:35.100
in the New England Journal of Medicine a couple years ago,

2912
02:03:35.100 --> 02:03:37.200
they actually had to stop the study early

2913
02:03:37.200 --> 02:03:39.870
because the group that received the stool transplant

2914
02:03:39.870 --> 02:03:43.110
did so much better than the group that received antibiotics,

2915
02:03:43.110 --> 02:03:44.490
and when you think about it,

2916
02:03:44.490 --> 02:03:47.190
a problem that's caused by antibiotics,

2917
02:03:47.190 --> 02:03:48.870
the likelihood you're gonna have, you know,

2918
02:03:48.870 --> 02:03:53.580
some fantastic solution from antibiotics is pretty low.

2919
02:03:53.580 --> 02:03:57.960
but FMT, fecal microbiota transplant, the technical term,

2920
02:03:57.960 --> 02:04:00.508
was shown to be vastly superior

2921
02:04:00.508 --> 02:04:02.850
to the antibiotic treatment, vancomycin,

2922
02:04:02.850 --> 02:04:05.700
that had here to fore been the standard for this,

2923
02:04:05.700 --> 02:04:07.440
and the reason that it works so well is

2924
02:04:07.440 --> 02:04:09.690
because that's an acute problem.

2925
02:04:09.690 --> 02:04:12.240
Your healthy bacteria have been killed off

2926
02:04:12.240 --> 02:04:13.470
by this dose of antibiotics.

2927
02:04:13.470 --> 02:04:16.740
You've acquired this bacteria, and we're gonna crowd it out

2928
02:04:16.740 --> 02:04:18.278
with a lot of other healthy bacteria,

2929
02:04:18.278 --> 02:04:20.970
and it's gonna take care of the problem.

2930
02:04:20.970 --> 02:04:23.250
When we look at more chronic problems

2931
02:04:23.250 --> 02:04:25.378
like autoimmune diseases, like a problem of Crohn's disease,

2932
02:04:25.378 --> 02:04:28.119
or ulcerative colitis, or autism, or Parkinson's,

2933
02:04:28.119 --> 02:04:29.670
or a lot of these things

2934
02:04:29.670 --> 02:04:34.670
that we're looking to FMT as a solution.

2935
02:04:34.950 --> 02:04:36.063
I think the biggest problem is

2936
02:04:36.063 --> 02:04:38.670
that the studies have been short-term.

2937
02:04:38.670 --> 02:04:40.721
You're not going to cure a chronic disease

2938
02:04:40.721 --> 02:04:43.052
giving a stool transplant for a week,

2939
02:04:43.052 --> 02:04:45.660
or two weeks, or maybe even two months,

2940
02:04:45.660 --> 02:04:47.816
and the other problem is, we're not looking at

2941
02:04:47.816 --> 02:04:50.250
what we're feeding those gut microbes.

2942
02:04:50.250 --> 02:04:52.426
So, there was an autism study looking at

2943
02:04:52.426 --> 02:04:55.740
FMT in conjunction with diet over several months,

2944
02:04:55.740 --> 02:04:57.360
and that study really stood out

2945
02:04:57.360 --> 02:04:58.930
head and shoulders above the other studies,

2946
02:04:58.930 --> 02:05:02.670
whereas FMT alone, and the same in the GI world,

2947
02:05:02.670 --> 02:05:05.300
just a few weeks ago, I was actually on vacation in Turkey,

2948
02:05:05.300 --> 02:05:06.720
and so, the time, you know,

2949
02:05:06.720 --> 02:05:09.120
we were seven hours ahead from DC,

2950
02:05:09.120 --> 02:05:10.785
and one of my GI colleagues, you know,

2951
02:05:10.785 --> 02:05:12.510
we all geek out on this stuff,

2952
02:05:12.510 --> 02:05:14.026
texted me, and it was, like, three in the morning,

2953
02:05:14.026 --> 02:05:16.314
or something, about this study

2954
02:05:16.314 --> 02:05:21.150
that just came out from Johns Hopkins

2955
02:05:21.150 --> 02:05:23.520
finding that clostridium difficile infection

2956
02:05:23.520 --> 02:05:26.550
was actually potentially linked to colon cancer,

2957
02:05:26.550 --> 02:05:29.400
and he's a person, my colleague at Georgetown,

2958
02:05:29.400 --> 02:05:30.780
really great guy, Mark Matar,

2959
02:05:30.780 --> 02:05:32.300
who does a lot of the fecal transplants,

2960
02:05:32.300 --> 02:05:33.330
and so, I saw it,

2961
02:05:33.330 --> 02:05:36.390
and then we immediately started this text exchange

2962
02:05:36.390 --> 02:05:38.850
and my husband was like, "It's 3:00 A.M. in Turkey,"

2963
02:05:38.850 --> 02:05:41.130
but the reason it was so exciting for us is

2964
02:05:41.130 --> 02:05:43.890
it links the antibiotic use,

2965
02:05:43.890 --> 02:05:45.630
because, why do people get C. diff?

2966
02:05:45.630 --> 02:05:47.790
The vast majority, it's from antibiotics.

2967
02:05:47.790 --> 02:05:50.160
You have some more sporadic or community acquired,

2968
02:05:50.160 --> 02:05:52.516
but it's primarily related to this antibiotic use

2969
02:05:52.516 --> 02:05:54.420
and maybe exposure in the hospital,

2970
02:05:54.420 --> 02:05:57.303
and so, this is a potential link

2971
02:05:57.303 --> 02:05:59.370
to antibiotic use and malignancy,

2972
02:05:59.370 --> 02:06:02.190
which is something that I've worried about for a long time,

2973
02:06:02.190 --> 02:06:06.030
but, again, it speaks to the acuity of that process.

2974
02:06:06.030 --> 02:06:08.010
You're trying to cure ulcerative colitis,

2975
02:06:08.010 --> 02:06:10.380
or put it into remission, or Crohn's disease,

2976
02:06:10.380 --> 02:06:11.760
that's a long-term process.

2977
02:06:11.760 --> 02:06:15.300
Those stool microbes for C. diff,

2978
02:06:15.300 --> 02:06:18.060
you're able to control the acute infection,

2979
02:06:18.060 --> 02:06:19.633
and then the microbiome reverts back

2980
02:06:19.633 --> 02:06:23.370
to something closer to baseline gradually over time,

2981
02:06:23.370 --> 02:06:26.640
but with colitis, or Crohn's, or MS, or Parkinson's,

2982
02:06:26.640 --> 02:06:29.370
you need the microbiome, those changes to last,

2983
02:06:29.370 --> 02:06:31.590
and so, in the studies where they combined it with diet,

2984
02:06:31.590 --> 02:06:33.430
they saw very different results.

2985
02:06:33.430 --> 02:06:36.510
It was another study that just came out just a few weeks ago

2986
02:06:36.510 --> 02:06:37.829
and this was a small study from India

2987
02:06:37.829 --> 02:06:39.877
looking at ulcerative colitis,

2988
02:06:39.877 --> 02:06:44.877
and they did the FMT with dietary change.

2989
02:06:45.120 --> 02:06:47.700
Very dramatic results, very different results,

2990
02:06:47.700 --> 02:06:51.060
much higher rates of response and of remission.

2991
02:06:51.060 --> 02:06:54.030
So, it's intuitive, Rich, that you've gotta continue to...

2992
02:06:54.030 --> 02:06:56.840
you can't feed the microbes Doritos and cheeseburgers.

2993
02:06:56.840 --> 02:06:59.304
Yeah, it's like plant a seed in the sand

2994
02:06:59.304 --> 02:07:00.810
and walking away from it,

2995
02:07:00.810 --> 02:07:03.120
and being disappointed that it doesn't grow

2996
02:07:03.120 --> 02:07:06.122
versus watering it, and feeding it,

2997
02:07:06.122 --> 02:07:08.066
and tilling the soil, and all of that.

2998
02:07:08.066 --> 02:07:11.346
I mean, conceivably, it makes sense

2999
02:07:11.346 --> 02:07:13.940
that even with a chronic condition,

3000
02:07:13.940 --> 02:07:15.990
you would be able to resolve it

3001
02:07:15.990 --> 02:07:18.488
if you had periodic fecal transplants

3002
02:07:18.488 --> 02:07:23.488
on some kind of schedule over a very extended period of time

3003
02:07:23.700 --> 02:07:25.830
and a very specific diet

3004
02:07:25.830 --> 02:07:30.013
that is intended to fertilize those transplants,

3005
02:07:31.315 --> 02:07:33.060
so that they're allowed to flourish,

3006
02:07:33.060 --> 02:07:36.900
and grow until they become sort of self-sufficient.

3007
02:07:36.900 --> 02:07:39.060
And the other thing we have to consider is,

3008
02:07:39.060 --> 02:07:42.450
what is the quality of the stool that's being transplanted?

3009
02:07:42.450 --> 02:07:44.040
So, is the stool from somebody

3010
02:07:44.040 --> 02:07:46.650
who's eaten at McDonald's every day?

3011
02:07:46.650 --> 02:07:49.620
The vetting process is very basic currently.

3012
02:07:49.620 --> 02:07:51.750
So, they exclude certain infectious diseases.

3013
02:07:51.750 --> 02:07:53.639
They test for syphilis, and HIV, and hepatitis,

3014
02:07:53.639 --> 02:07:56.629
and various things, and there's certain conditions

3015
02:07:56.629 --> 02:07:58.980
that will exclude you

3016
02:07:58.980 --> 02:08:02.190
from being able to be a donor, and medications,

3017
02:08:02.190 --> 02:08:03.300
but there's nothing about,

3018
02:08:03.300 --> 02:08:06.030
I want to know like how many vegetables do you eat?

3019
02:08:06.030 --> 02:08:09.723
I'm seeing a new product category, you know, on Goop.

3020
02:08:11.270 --> 02:08:12.103
I mean, I always said,

3021
02:08:12.103 --> 02:08:14.640
if I were getting a stool transplant,

3022
02:08:14.640 --> 02:08:17.220
I would be going to Tanzania, and getting some stool

3023
02:08:17.220 --> 02:08:19.990
from one of my Hadza tribe brothers or sisters.

3024
02:08:19.990 --> 02:08:22.599
I don't want stool from somebody

3025
02:08:22.599 --> 02:08:24.270
who's been eating at McDonald's every day.

3026
02:08:24.270 --> 02:08:28.530
So, in the studies where they looked at the quality,

3027
02:08:28.530 --> 02:08:32.095
the microbial richness, diversity, robustness of the stool,

3028
02:08:32.095 --> 02:08:34.200
you measure short chain fatty acids.

3029
02:08:34.200 --> 02:08:35.100
That's night and day.

3030
02:08:35.100 --> 02:08:36.600
It's like any other transplant.

3031
02:08:36.600 --> 02:08:37.860
If you're getting heart transplant

3032
02:08:37.860 --> 02:08:42.110
from an older person who lived a very unhealthy life,

3033
02:08:42.110 --> 02:08:44.125
you're gonna have a different outcome,

3034
02:08:44.125 --> 02:08:45.870
if you're getting at heart transplant

3035
02:08:45.870 --> 02:08:47.730
from a young, healthy person,

3036
02:08:47.730 --> 02:08:51.140
and in this case, I would say age, maybe less so,

3037
02:08:51.140 --> 02:08:54.183
but because I would take some microbes from Mike Fremont.

3038
02:08:55.860 --> 02:08:57.426
Yeah, Mike, could support himself

3039
02:08:57.426 --> 02:08:59.970
for the rest of his life selling his--

3040
02:08:59.970 --> 02:09:01.530
Selling his stool, yeah.

3041
02:09:01.530 --> 02:09:02.910
So, we have to look at, that's great,

3042
02:09:02.910 --> 02:09:04.560
what are we feeding the microbes

3043
02:09:04.560 --> 02:09:06.360
that are being transplanted,

3044
02:09:06.360 --> 02:09:09.570
and what is the quality of the transplanted stool?

3045
02:09:09.570 --> 02:09:12.210
And I think in the future, we'll see some new innovations.

3046
02:09:12.210 --> 02:09:14.640
So, we'll see, instead of somebody else's stool,

3047
02:09:14.640 --> 02:09:17.400
where we do have to worry about infectious things,

3048
02:09:17.400 --> 02:09:19.922
and that's why getting stool from an intimate contact

3049
02:09:19.922 --> 02:09:21.630
can make it more convenient.

3050
02:09:21.630 --> 02:09:24.180
You're already swapping body fluids,

3051
02:09:24.180 --> 02:09:27.270
but there may be issues with the quality of the stool there

3052
02:09:27.270 --> 02:09:30.780
that are not ideal is, can we amplify our own bacteria?

3053
02:09:30.780 --> 02:09:33.567
Can we take our existing faecalibacterium prausnitzii,

3054
02:09:33.567 --> 02:09:36.060
and maybe take them out of the body,

3055
02:09:36.060 --> 02:09:37.465
and amplify them in some way,

3056
02:09:37.465 --> 02:09:39.180
maybe with an ideal growth medium,

3057
02:09:39.180 --> 02:09:40.820
and then reintroduce them,

3058
02:09:40.820 --> 02:09:43.250
and, of course, Rich, we can do that with food.

3059
02:09:43.250 --> 02:09:44.580
We could do that with food.

3060
02:09:44.580 --> 02:09:46.560
That's the great thing is we don't have to sit around

3061
02:09:46.560 --> 02:09:48.510
and wait for these moon shots

3062
02:09:48.510 --> 02:09:51.750
to become accessible to all of us.

3063
02:09:51.750 --> 02:09:53.790
There are all these things that we can do every single day.

3064
02:09:53.790 --> 02:09:55.140
It's back to the original point

3065
02:09:55.140 --> 02:09:56.720
that we opened this conversation with,

3066
02:09:56.720 --> 02:10:00.570
which is the resilience of the human body

3067
02:10:00.570 --> 02:10:03.979
and the ability of the microbiome to adapt,

3068
02:10:03.979 --> 02:10:06.030
and, you know, throughout your book,

3069
02:10:06.030 --> 02:10:07.260
I mean, as you mentioned,

3070
02:10:07.260 --> 02:10:10.230
a third of the book really is this plan.

3071
02:10:10.230 --> 02:10:11.820
You've laid out this entire plan.

3072
02:10:11.820 --> 02:10:15.180
It's about mastering your mind, securing defenses,

3073
02:10:15.180 --> 02:10:18.510
changing your environment, rethinking these therapeutics,

3074
02:10:18.510 --> 02:10:20.280
and you walk people through it step by step,

3075
02:10:20.280 --> 02:10:23.360
very practical things that every one of us can do,

3076
02:10:23.360 --> 02:10:26.550
including all these recipes for different kinds of foods

3077
02:10:26.550 --> 02:10:31.290
that are specific to optimizing the health of our gut,

3078
02:10:31.290 --> 02:10:33.777
and I'm excited for people to read it.

3079
02:10:33.777 --> 02:10:36.060
I think it's really powerful,

3080
02:10:36.060 --> 02:10:38.040
and it's empowering, and that's the point.

3081
02:10:38.040 --> 02:10:39.848
It's not just an academic pursuit

3082
02:10:39.848 --> 02:10:43.380
to try to understand the microbiome,

3083
02:10:43.380 --> 02:10:45.150
but to really give people tools

3084
02:10:45.150 --> 02:10:47.160
that they can implement into their lives

3085
02:10:47.160 --> 02:10:49.860
to take better control of their health trajectory.

3086
02:10:49.860 --> 02:10:52.560
And I think it is an optimistic message.

3087
02:10:52.560 --> 02:10:56.730
I find it reassuring, this information.

3088
02:10:56.730 --> 02:11:01.170
There's stuff you can do, because, the fear mongering,

3089
02:11:01.170 --> 02:11:04.193
you know, and that's how you sell stuff to people is

3090
02:11:04.193 --> 02:11:05.580
you make 'em afraid, right?

3091
02:11:05.580 --> 02:11:06.880
And I'm not interested in doing that.

3092
02:11:06.880 --> 02:11:09.300
I'm interested in making sure people

3093
02:11:09.300 --> 02:11:11.700
have the information to empower themselves,

3094
02:11:11.700 --> 02:11:13.440
so that they don't need my help, quite frankly.

3095
02:11:13.440 --> 02:11:18.390
Yeah, and if somebody is looking for your kind of help,

3096
02:11:18.390 --> 02:11:21.270
maybe they're not able to come and see you,

3097
02:11:21.270 --> 02:11:25.140
but what is a way or a resource that's available to people

3098
02:11:25.140 --> 02:11:26.579
who are trying to find someone

3099
02:11:26.579 --> 02:11:30.487
who has your type of specialty in an integrative sense,

3100
02:11:30.487 --> 02:11:32.733
in their respective area?

3101
02:11:33.660 --> 02:11:35.823
Yeah that's--
Is there a way to do that?

3102
02:11:35.823 --> 02:11:37.080
That a tough one, Rich,

3103
02:11:37.080 --> 02:11:39.570
we don't currently have

3104
02:11:39.570 --> 02:11:42.480
an association of integrative gastroenterologists,

3105
02:11:42.480 --> 02:11:45.420
although Will B., and myself, and Jerry Mullen,

3106
02:11:45.420 --> 02:11:50.420
and this great guy, Desmond, a British gastroenterologist,

3107
02:11:51.030 --> 02:11:54.060
who I met at ICNM, I'm blanking on his last name,

3108
02:11:54.060 --> 02:11:57.835
but he runs a clinic in the UK treating patients with IBD,

3109
02:11:57.835 --> 02:11:59.160
plant-based diet.

3110
02:11:59.160 --> 02:12:00.138
So, there's a handful of us,

3111
02:12:00.138 --> 02:12:01.890
and we've been chatting about it,

3112
02:12:01.890 --> 02:12:06.360
and the idea wouldn't be,

3113
02:12:06.360 --> 02:12:08.760
you have to be a plant-based physician to be part of this.

3114
02:12:08.760 --> 02:12:09.938
Not at all, it's just this idea

3115
02:12:09.938 --> 02:12:12.570
of people who believe in the concept of food as medicine

3116
02:12:12.570 --> 02:12:14.850
for treating digestive disorders.

3117
02:12:14.850 --> 02:12:19.620
So, I think that you've gotta ask some questions,

3118
02:12:19.620 --> 02:12:21.534
and you might have a gastroenterologist

3119
02:12:21.534 --> 02:12:24.420
who maybe isn't particularly focused on this,

3120
02:12:24.420 --> 02:12:26.400
but is open to this, and maybe you're working

3121
02:12:26.400 --> 02:12:29.730
with a nutritionist, and the gastroenterologist is open,

3122
02:12:29.730 --> 02:12:34.020
and I think, you know, my evolution from a more...

3123
02:12:34.020 --> 02:12:35.940
I'm a conventionally trained gastroenterologist,

3124
02:12:35.940 --> 02:12:38.537
but from somebody who wrote a lot of prescriptions,

3125
02:12:38.537 --> 02:12:41.460
and practiced more conventional medicine,

3126
02:12:41.460 --> 02:12:44.730
to somebody who now believes fervently

3127
02:12:44.730 --> 02:12:46.320
in the power of food as medicine,

3128
02:12:46.320 --> 02:12:48.073
and what the individual can do

3129
02:12:48.073 --> 02:12:52.430
in addition to prescribing medication when needed.

3130
02:12:52.430 --> 02:12:54.900
That evolution really came

3131
02:12:54.900 --> 02:12:57.480
because of the patients who were generous enough

3132
02:12:57.480 --> 02:12:58.687
to share what they were doing with me

3133
02:12:58.687 --> 02:13:00.967
when I didn't know, when I was like,

3134
02:13:00.967 --> 02:13:02.640
"Really, that stuff works?"

3135
02:13:02.640 --> 02:13:03.473
And they were like, "Yeah, this is..."

3136
02:13:03.473 --> 02:13:05.257
I'm like, "How is it that you're...

3137
02:13:05.257 --> 02:13:06.697
"I scoped you, and your ulcers

3138
02:13:06.697 --> 02:13:07.724
"are all gone from your colitis.

3139
02:13:07.724 --> 02:13:09.120
"What are you doing?"

3140
02:13:09.120 --> 02:13:10.477
And they'd be like, "Well, as a matter of fact,

3141
02:13:10.477 --> 02:13:11.310
"this is what I'm doing.

3142
02:13:11.310 --> 02:13:13.580
"I've changed my diet, I'm doing this, I'm focusing on..."

3143
02:13:13.580 --> 02:13:18.580
And so, it was really a patient led evolution,

3144
02:13:19.020 --> 02:13:21.360
and so, I always tell people, like,

3145
02:13:21.360 --> 02:13:22.921
don't break up with your gastroenterologist

3146
02:13:22.921 --> 02:13:25.050
unless they're just really an asshole,

3147
02:13:25.050 --> 02:13:28.710
in which case, absolutely do, but bring them along.

3148
02:13:28.710 --> 02:13:31.020
So, maybe you find somebody,

3149
02:13:31.020 --> 02:13:33.660
a nutritionist who's well versed, who can help,

3150
02:13:33.660 --> 02:13:35.736
and you make sure that that gastroenterologist is

3151
02:13:35.736 --> 02:13:37.587
open to the idea of what you're gonna do,

3152
02:13:37.587 --> 02:13:40.830
and I always say, tell them, like, don't hide.

3153
02:13:40.830 --> 02:13:43.597
Like, say, "Look, I know you want me to take this drug,

3154
02:13:43.597 --> 02:13:46.327
"this biologic, and I'm aware that this could help me,

3155
02:13:46.327 --> 02:13:48.457
"but I'd like to try something else right now

3156
02:13:48.457 --> 02:13:50.410
"and I feel like my disease is sufficiently controlled,

3157
02:13:50.410 --> 02:13:51.924
"that that's safe for me to do."

3158
02:13:51.924 --> 02:13:53.550
You know, "Do you agree?"

3159
02:13:53.550 --> 02:13:54.660
And this is what I'm gonna do.

3160
02:13:54.660 --> 02:13:57.870
I'm gonna try this diet, and I'm gonna start meditating,

3161
02:13:57.870 --> 02:14:00.030
and going for a walk in the woods, and whatever else,

3162
02:14:00.030 --> 02:14:04.740
and we'll see how it goes, and if it doesn't work,

3163
02:14:04.740 --> 02:14:06.810
I'm gonna come back and get that drug

3164
02:14:06.810 --> 02:14:08.550
that you're telling me could help,

3165
02:14:08.550 --> 02:14:10.853
even though it could potentially cause an infection

3166
02:14:10.853 --> 02:14:14.100
and give me cancer, because I think I need it at that point,

3167
02:14:14.100 --> 02:14:15.330
but I'm gonna explore this stuff.

3168
02:14:15.330 --> 02:14:19.200
So, I think the way we change a medical community is

3169
02:14:19.200 --> 02:14:21.030
we have to change it from the inside,

3170
02:14:21.030 --> 02:14:23.490
and if everybody abandons their doctor

3171
02:14:23.490 --> 02:14:25.440
and seeks somebody else out, that's not gonna work.

3172
02:14:25.440 --> 02:14:27.590
So, bring your doctor along,

3173
02:14:27.590 --> 02:14:31.860
help to open his or her eyes about what can be done

3174
02:14:31.860 --> 02:14:35.430
and create those bridges between the other practitioners

3175
02:14:35.430 --> 02:14:36.630
that you were using,

3176
02:14:36.630 --> 02:14:39.450
or it may even be a book that you've read.

3177
02:14:39.450 --> 02:14:42.286
Books are fantastic, but you really want to find

3178
02:14:42.286 --> 02:14:45.420
that practitioner who can be on that journey with you.

3179
02:14:45.420 --> 02:14:46.980
I think that's a really important part of it.

3180
02:14:46.980 --> 02:14:48.780
Beautifully put, the communication,

3181
02:14:48.780 --> 02:14:51.330
